You can solve this problem using the Java stream API by first sorting the input list and then finding the first element that appears more than once in the sorted list. Here's an example implementation:
```
import java.util.Arrays;
import java.util.List;

public class Solution {
  public static int findMostFrequentElement(List<Integer> input) {
    // sort the input list
    List<Integer> sortedInput = Arrays.asList(input).stream().sorted().collect(Collectors.toList());

    // find the first element that appears more than once in the sorted list
    OptionalInt mostFrequentElement = sortedInput.stream()
      .filter(i -> i > 0)
      .mapToObj(Integer::new)
      .collect(() -> new HashSet<>(), (set, el) -> {
        if (!set.contains(el)) {
          set.add(el);
        } else {
          set.remove(el);
        }
      })
      .stream()
      .findFirst();

    // return the most frequent element, or -1 if no element appears more than once
    return mostFrequentElement.orElse(-1);
  }
}
```
This implementation first sorts the input list using the `sorted()` method of the stream API. It then filters out all elements that are equal to zero, since they do not have any frequency information. The remaining elements are then converted to a set using the `collect` method with a custom accumulator function. The accumulator function adds or removes elements from the set based on whether they appear more than once in the input list. Finally, the most frequent element is found by finding the first element that appears more than once in the sorted list. If no element appears more than once, then -1 is returned.

