import com.github.programmerr47.singleinstances.InstanceStorage.HierarchyLink;
```

You need to implement the following method:
```java
public class HierarchyUtil {
    public static HierarchyLink findHierarchyLink(Class<?> class1, Class<?> class2) {
        // Your implementation here
    }
}
```

The `HierarchyLink` class represents a link in the class hierarchy and has the following structure:
```java
public class HierarchyLink {
    private Class<?> parentClass;
    private Class<?> childClass;

    public HierarchyLink(Class<?> parentClass, Class<?> childClass) {
        this.parentClass = parentClass;
        this.childClass = childClass;
    }

    // Getters and setters
}
```

Your task is to implement the `findHierarchyLink` method to return a `HierarchyLink` object representing the link between the two given classes. If there is no direct or indirect hierarchy link between the classes, the method should return `null`.

For example, if `class1` is a direct parent of `class2`, the method should return a `HierarchyLink` object with `class1` as the parent and `class2` as the child.

response:
The `findHierarchyLink` method can be implemented using the `Class.isAssignableFrom()` method to determine if one class is a parent of another. Here's an example implementation:
```java
public static HierarchyLink findHierarchyLink(Class<?> class1, Class<?> class2) {
    // Check if class1 is a parent of class2
    if (class1.isAssignableFrom(class2)) {
        return new HierarchyLink(class1, class2);
    }

    // Check if class1 is an interface and any of its implementations is a parent of class2
    for (Class<?> implementedInterface : class1.getInterfaces()) {
        if (implementedInterface.isAssignableFrom(class2)) {
            return new HierarchyLink(class1, class2);
        }
    }

    // Check if class1 is a parent of any superclass of class2
    Class<?> superClass = class2.getSuperclass();
    while (superClass != null) {
        if (class1.isAssignableFrom(superClass)) {
            return new HierarchyLink(class1, class2);
        }
        superClass = superClass.getSuperclass();
    }

    // No direct or indirect hierarchy link found
    return null;
}
```
This method first checks if `class1` is a parent of `class2` using the `isAssignableFrom()` method. If it returns true, then there is a direct hierarchy link between the two classes and the method returns a `HierarchyLink` object with `class1` as the parent and `class2` as the child.

If `class1` is not a parent of `class2`, the method checks if `class1` is an interface and any of its implementations is a parent of `class2`. If it finds such an implementation, then there is an indirect hierarchy link between the two classes and the method returns a `HierarchyLink` object with `class1` as the parent and `class2` as the child.

Finally, if no direct or indirect hierarchy link is found, the method returns null.

