To design a program using Node.js and MongoDB that sorts the output based on the economic value and allows users to modify the economic estimation threshold dynamically, you can follow these steps:

1. Define the data model for your program. In this case, it would be an object with the following properties:
```json
{
  "id": ObjectId("..."),
  "name": String,
  "economic_estimation": Number,
  "description": String
}
```
2. Create a MongoDB collection to store the data. You can use the `mongoose` library in Node.js to interact with your MongoDB database.
3. Define a function that retrieves all the data from the MongoDB collection and sorts it based on the economic estimation value in descending order.
4. Allow users to modify the economic estimation threshold dynamically by using a configuration file or an API endpoint. You can use the `config` library in Node.js to read the configuration file and store the new threshold value in a variable.
5. Use the `find()` method of the MongoDB collection to retrieve all the data from the database, then filter the results based on the economic estimation threshold using the `filter()` method.
6. Use the `sort()` method of the filtered results to sort them based on the economic estimation value in descending order.
7. Limit the results to the top 10 by using the `limit()` method of the sorted results.
8. Return the limited results to the user.

Here is an example of how you can implement this program:
```javascript
const mongoose = require('mongoose');
const config = require('config');

// Define the data model for your program
const DataModel = new mongoose.Schema({
  name: String,
  economic_estimation: Number,
  description: String
});

// Create a MongoDB collection to store the data
const DataCollection = mongoose.model('Data', DataModel);

// Define a function that retrieves all the data from the MongoDB collection and sorts it based on the economic estimation value in descending order
function getSortedResults(threshold) {
  // Retrieve all the data from the MongoDB collection
  const data = await DataCollection.find();

  // Filter the results based on the economic estimation threshold
  const filteredData = data.filter((item) => item.economic_estimation >= threshold);

  // Sort the filtered results based on the economic estimation value in descending order
  const sortedResults = filteredData.sort((a, b) => b.economic_estimation - a.economic_estimation);

  // Limit the results to the top 10
  const limitedResults = sortedResults.limit(10);

  return limitedResults;
}

// Allow users to modify the economic estimation threshold dynamically by using a configuration file or an API endpoint
const threshold = config.get('threshold');

// Retrieve the top 10 results from the MongoDB collection, sorted based on the economic estimation value in descending order
const results = getSortedResults(threshold);

console.log(results);
```
In this example, we define a data model for our program that includes a name, an economic estimation, and a description field. We then create a MongoDB collection to store the data using the `mongoose` library.

We define a function called `getSortedResults()` that retrieves all the data from the MongoDB collection, filters it based on the economic estimation threshold, sorts it in descending order based on the economic estimation value, and limits the results to the top 10. We also allow users to modify the economic estimation threshold dynamically by using a configuration file or an API endpoint.

To use this program, you can run the `getSortedResults()` function and pass in the desired threshold as a parameter, like this:
```javascript
const results = getSortedResults(100); // Retrieve the top 10 results from the MongoDB collection, sorted based on the economic estimation value in descending order with a threshold of $100.
```
You can also modify the threshold dynamically by using a configuration file or an API endpoint to update the value of the `threshold` variable.

