Here is one possible implementation of the `replaceSubstring` function in C++:
```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <string>

std::string replaceSubstring(const std::string& original, const std::string& from, const std::string& to) {
    std::string modified = original;
    size_t pos = 0;
    while ((pos = modified.find(from, pos)) != std::string::npos) {
        modified.replace(pos, from.length(), to);
        pos += to.length();
    }
    return modified;
}
```
This function takes in three parameters: the original string `original`, the substring to be replaced `from`, and the replacement substring `to`. It returns a modified string with all occurrences of `from` replaced with `to`.

The function starts by creating a copy of the original string, which is stored in the variable `modified`. The variable `pos` is initialized to 0, indicating that we are at the beginning of the string.

The while loop then searches for the first occurrence of `from` in the modified string using the `find` method. If an occurrence is found, the `replace` method is used to replace it with the replacement substring `to`. The position of the next search is advanced by the length of the replacement substring using the `+=` operator.

The loop continues until no more occurrences of `from` are found in the modified string. Once the loop finishes, the modified string is returned.

Here's an example usage of this function:
```cpp
int main() {
    std::string original = "hello world hello";
    std::string from = "hello";
    std::string to = "hi";
    std::string modified = replaceSubstring(original, from, to);
    std::cout << modified << std::endl; // Output: hi world hi
    return 0;
}
```

