Here's one possible solution:
```
class KthLargest {
    private final PriorityQueue<Integer> pq;
    private final int k;

    public KthLargest(int k, int[] nums) {
        this.k = k;
        this.pq = new PriorityQueue<>();
        for (int num : nums) {
            this.add(num);
        }
    }

    public int add(int val) {
        if (this.pq.size() < this.k) {
            this.pq.offer(val);
        } else if (val > pq.peek()) {
            this.pq.remove();
            this.pq.offer(val);
        }
        return this.pq.peek();
    }

    public void remove(int val) {
        if (this.pq.contains(val)) {
            this.pq.remove(val);
        }
    }
}
```
Explanation:
The solution uses a `PriorityQueue` to keep track of the largest `k` elements in the stream. The `add` method adds the element to the queue if it's not full, or replaces the smallest element with the new one if the queue is already full. The `remove` method removes the first occurrence of the specified value from the queue.

The `KthLargest` class has two main methods: `add` and `remove`. The `add` method takes an integer value as input and returns the current kth largest element in the stream. If the queue is not full, it adds the value to the queue and returns the current kth largest element. If the queue is already full, it replaces the smallest element with the new one if the new value is larger than the current smallest element, and returns the current kth largest element.

The `remove` method takes an integer value as input and removes the first occurrence of the specified value from the queue. If the specified value is not in the queue, it does nothing.

The constructor initializes the `PriorityQueue` with the given array of integers and the k value. The `add` and `remove` methods use the `pq` object to add or remove elements from the queue.

