@Entity
public class CartItem {
    @Id
    @GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.IDENTITY)
    private Long id;

    @OneToOne(fetch = FetchType.LAZY, cascade = CascadeType.ALL)
    @JoinColumn(name = "id_carrito")
    private ProductModel productModel;

    private Double subTotal;
    private Double quantity;

    // getters and setters
}
```
This is the design of the `CartItem` class, which has the specified attributes and annotations to fulfill the requirements of a shopping cart item in a database-driven application.

The `@Entity` annotation indicates that this is an entity class, which means it will be persisted to the database.

The `@Id` annotation specifies that the `id` field is the primary key for this class, and it will be generated automatically by the database using the `GenerationType.IDENTITY` strategy.

The `@OneToOne` annotation establishes a one-to-one relationship between the `CartItem` class and the `ProductModel` class. The `fetch = FetchType.LAZY` specifies that the product model should be fetched lazily, meaning it will only be loaded when it is accessed for the first time. The `cascade = CascadeType.ALL` specifies that all operations on the cart item should also cascade to the associated product model.

The `@JoinColumn(name = "id_carrito")` annotation specifies that the `idCarrito` field in the `ProductModel` class is the foreign key for the relationship between the two classes. The `name` parameter specifies the name of the foreign key column in the `ProductModel` table.

The `subTotal` and `quantity` fields are used to store the subtotal cost and quantity of the item, respectively. They are both declared as `Double` attributes, which allows for precise floating-point numbers to be stored.

Finally, getters and setters are included for each attribute to allow for convenient access and modification of the class's fields.

