```
// This function takes two sorted arrays as input and returns a sorted array that contains all the elements of the two input arrays.
// The optimization is done by using a single sort instead of multiple sorts.
function sortedMerge(arr1, arr2) {
  // First, check if either of the input arrays is empty. If so, return the other array.
  if (arr1.length === 0) {
    return arr2;
  } else if (arr2.length === 0) {
    return arr1;
  }

  // Create a new array that will contain all the elements of both input arrays.
  let result = [];

  // Initialize two pointers, one for each input array.
  let i = 0;
  let j = 0;

  // Loop through both input arrays until one of them is empty.
  while (i < arr1.length && j < arr2.length) {
    // Compare the current elements of the two input arrays and add the smaller one to the result array.
    if (arr1[i] <= arr2[j]) {
      result.push(arr1[i]);
      i++;
    } else {
      result.push(arr2[j]);
      j++;
    }
  }

  // Add any remaining elements from the input arrays to the result array.
  while (i < arr1.length) {
    result.push(arr1[i++]);
  }
  while (j < arr2.length) {
    result.push(arr2[j++]);
  }

  // Return the sorted result array.
  return result;
}
```
In this optimized version of the function, we use a single sort to merge the two input arrays instead of multiple sorts. This reduces the number of comparisons and iterations required, making the function faster and more efficient. Additionally, we added comments to make the code more readable and easier to understand.

