You are correct that the current implementation of `isPrime` is not very efficient and can become computationally expensive for large inputs. There are several ways to optimize the algorithm, but one common approach is to use the Sieve of Eratosthenes, which is a simple and efficient method for finding all prime numbers up to a given number.

Here's an example implementation of the Sieve of Eratosthenes in JavaScript:
```
function getPrimes(n) {
  const primes = [];
  const sieve = new Array(n + 1).fill(true);

  for (let p = 2; p <= Math.sqrt(n); p++) {
    if (sieve[p]) {
      for (let i = p * p; i <= n; i += p) {
        sieve[i] = false;
      }
    }
  }

  return primes.concat(sieve.map((v, i) => i).filter(v => v));
}
```
This implementation uses an array `sieve` to keep track of which numbers have been marked as composite, and it iterates over the numbers in the range `[2, n]` to mark them as prime or composite based on their divisibility. Finally, it returns a list of all prime numbers up to `n`.

Using this optimized implementation, you can check if a number is prime by calling `getPrimes(number)` and checking if the number is in the returned list.
```
function main() {
  const number = 1234;
  const primes = getPrimes(number);

  console.log(primes.includes(number)); // true or false
}
```

