Here is a Java program that finds the mean and median of n numbers using a divide and conquer approach with a time complexity of O(n log n) and a space complexity of O(n):
```
import java.util.Arrays;

public class FindMeanAndMedian {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] numbers = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5}; // Example input array
        int n = numbers.length;
        double mean = findMean(numbers, n);
        int median = findMedian(numbers, n);
        System.out.println("Mean: " + mean);
        System.out.println("Median: " + median);
    }

    private static double findMean(int[] numbers, int n) {
        // Divide the array into two halves and find the mean of each half
        if (n == 0) {
            return 0;
        } else if (n == 1) {
            return numbers[0];
        } else {
            int mid = n / 2;
            double leftMean = findMean(numbers, mid);
            double rightMean = findMean(numbers, n - mid);
            return (leftMean + rightMean) / 2.0;
        }
    }

    private static int findMedian(int[] numbers, int n) {
        // Divide the array into two halves and find the median of each half
        if (n == 0) {
            return 0;
        } else if (n == 1) {
            return numbers[0];
        } else {
            int mid = n / 2;
            int leftMedian = findMedian(numbers, mid);
            int rightMedian = findMedian(numbers, n - mid);
            return Math.min(leftMedian, rightMedian);
        }
    }
}
```
This program uses a divide and conquer approach to find the mean and median of an array of integers in O(n log n) time and O(n) space. The `findMean` method divides the array into two halves recursively until the length of each half is 1, and then finds the mean of each half. Similarly, the `findMedian` method divides the array into two halves recursively until the length of each half is 1, and then finds the median of each half. The `main` method calls these methods with the input array and its length to find the mean and median of the array.

The time complexity of this program is O(n log n) because the `findMean` method recursively divides the array into smaller halves until the length of each half is 1, which takes O(log n) time for each half. The `findMedian` method also recursively divides the array into smaller halves until the length of each half is 1, but it does not take as much time as the `findMean` method because it only needs to find the median of each half instead of finding the mean. Therefore, the overall time complexity of this program is O(n log n) for both methods.

The space complexity of this program is O(n) because it uses an array to store the input numbers and a recursive call stack to store the state of the computation. The size of the call stack depends on the length of the input array, which is O(n), so the overall space complexity is also O(n).

