Here is an example of how you might implement a custom sorting algorithm in JavaScript to sort a list of strings based on their lengths:
```
function sortByLength(strList) {
  // Initialize an empty array for the sorted list
  let sortedList = [];

  // Loop through each string in the input list and add it to the sorted list
  for (let i = 0; i < strList.length; i++) {
    let currentString = strList[i];
    let currentLength = currentString.length;

    // Find the index where the current string should be inserted in the sorted list
    let insertIndex = 0;
    while (insertIndex < sortedList.length && sortedList[insertIndex].length > currentLength) {
      insertIndex++;
    }

    // Insert the current string into the sorted list at its appropriate index
    sortedList.splice(insertIndex, 0, currentString);
  }

  return sortedList;
}
```
This algorithm has a time complexity of O(n^2) because it requires two nested loops to iterate through the input list and compare each string's length with those that have already been processed. The space complexity is O(1) because only one array (the sorted list) needs to be stored in memory during the sorting process.

It's worth noting that this algorithm is not as efficient as built-in sorting algorithms like quicksort or mergesort, which have a time complexity of O(n log n) on average. However, for small lists or lists with a small number of elements, this custom algorithm may be sufficient and more readable than using a built-in sort function.

Here is an optimized version of the `quicksort` function that uses a divide-and-conquer approach and takes advantage of the fact that arrays are homogeneous:
```scss
function quicksort(arr) {
  if (arr.length <= 1) return arr;

  // Select a pivot element from the array
  const pivot = arr[0];

  // Partition the array into two subarrays based on whether the elements are less than or greater than the pivot
  const lessThanPivot = arr.filter((elem) => elem < pivot);
  const greaterThanPivot = arr.filter((elem) => elem >= pivot);

  // Recursively sort the two subarrays
  return [...quicksort(lessThanPivot), pivot, ...quicksort(greaterThanPivot)];
}
```
This implementation of `quicksort` is more efficient because it only makes a single pass through the array and does not require any additional storage for temporary variables. Additionally, because arrays are homogeneous, we can use the `<` operator to compare elements, which simplifies the logic and reduces the number of comparisons needed.

Here is an optimized version of the `customSort` function that uses a divide-and-conquer approach and takes advantage of the fact that arrays are homogeneous:
```scss
function customSort(arr, compare) {
  if (arr.length <= 1) return arr;

  // Select a pivot element from the array
  const pivot = arr[0];

  // Partition the array into two subarrays based on whether the elements are less than or greater than the pivot
  const lessThanPivot = arr.filter((elem) => compare(elem, pivot) < 0);
  const greaterThanPivot = arr.filter((elem) => compare(elem, pivot) >= 0);

  // Recursively sort the two subarrays
  return [...customSort(lessThanPivot, compare), pivot, ...customSort(greaterThanPivot, compare)];
}
```
This implementation of `customSort` is more efficient because it only makes a single pass through the array and does not require any additional storage for temporary variables. Additionally, we can use the `compare` function to compare elements based on their values, which simplifies the logic and reduces the number of comparisons needed.

