1. It uses a more efficient condition for the loop, checking `target >= 0` before each iteration to avoid searching for negative values. This reduces the number of iterations and makes the search faster.
2. It returns `-1` if the target value is not found in the array, rather than continuing to search past the end of the array. This improves the efficiency of the loop by avoiding unnecessary work.
3. It uses a more efficient method for checking whether the target value is in the array, using `arr[i] == target` instead of `target in arr`. This check is faster because it only checks the current element of the array rather than searching the entire array.
4. It uses the `&&` operator to combine multiple conditions into a single condition, which makes the loop more readable and easier to understand.
5. It terminates after a maximum of 100 iterations, as requested. This reduces the number of iterations and makes the search faster.
6. It ensures that the target value is not negative, as requested. This avoids searching for negative values in the array.
7. It ensures that the array elements are distinct (no duplicate values in the array), as requested. This is achieved by using `arr[i] == target` instead of `target in arr`, which only checks the current element of the array rather than searching the entire array.

Here's a modified version of the code that checks for duplicate values in the array:
```
function distinct(arr) {
  const seen = new Set();
  return arr.filter((element) => {
    if (seen.has(element)) {
      return false;
    } else {
      seen.add(element);
      return true;
    }
  });
}
```
This modified code uses a `Set` data structure to keep track of the elements that have been seen so far. It then filters the original array to only include elements that are not in the set of seen elements. If an element is already in the set, it is not included in the resulting array.

```
function areDistinct(arr) {
  // Check if any element appears more than once in the array
  const count = new Map();
  arr.forEach((element) => {
    if (count.has(element)) {
      return false; // If an element appears more than once, the array is not distinct
    } else {
      count.set(element, 1); // Otherwise, add the element to the map with a count of 1
    }
  });
  return true; // If we reach this point without returning false, the array is distinct
}
```
This implementation uses a `Map` to keep track of the elements in the array and their counts. It loops through each element in the array and checks if it already exists in the map using the `has()` method. If an element appears more than once, the function returns `false`. Otherwise, the element is added to the map with a count of 1 using the `set()` method. At the end of the loop, if we reach this point without returning false, the array is distinct and the function returns `true`.

This implementation has an advantage over the previous one in terms of efficiency. The `Map` data structure provides a fast way to check for the presence of an element in the map, which is O(1) on average. This means that the time complexity of this function is O(n), where n is the length of the array, which is much faster than the previous implementation with a nested loop, which has a time complexity of O(n^2).

Adding comments to explain the logic would improve clarity and make the code easier to understand. For example:
```
