To implement the selection sort algorithm, we will follow these steps:

1. Start by assuming that the first element of the list is the smallest element.
2. Find the smallest element in the rest of the list and swap it with the current smallest element.
3. Repeat step 2 until all elements have been compared and swapped.

Here is the implementation of the selection sort algorithm for the list [8, 3, 6, 7, 1]:
```
// Assume that the first element is the smallest element
smallest = 0

// Loop through the rest of the list
for i in range(1, len(list)):
    // If the current element is smaller than the smallest element, update the smallest element and its index
    if list[i] < list[smallest]:
        smallest = i

// Swap the smallest element with the first element of the list
list[0], list[smallest] = list[smallest], list[0]
```
After running this code, the resulting list will be [1, 3, 6, 7, 8].

The minimum element in the sorted rotated array is 0. To find it, we can use the following algorithm:

1. Compare the first and last elements of the array. If they are equal, then the minimum element is the first element of the array. Otherwise, continue to step 2.
2. Find the pivot element in the array by searching for the first element that is greater than its previous element. The pivot element separates the sorted and rotated sections of the array.
3. Compare the pivot element with the middle element of the array. If the pivot element is less than the middle element, then the minimum element is in the left half of the array. Otherwise, it is in the right half.
4. Recursively apply step 3 to the appropriate half of the array until we find the minimum element.

Here's an example implementation in Java:
```
public int findMin(int[] arr) {
    if (arr[0] <= arr[arr.length - 1]) {
        return arr[0];
    }

    int pivot = findPivot(arr);
    int middle = arr.length / 2;

    if (pivot < arr[middle]) {
        return findMin(Arrays.copyOfRange(arr, 0, middle));
    } else {
        return findMin(Arrays.copyOfRange(arr, middle, arr.length));
    }
}

private int findPivot(int[] arr) {
    for (int i = 1; i < arr.length - 1; i++) {
        if (arr[i] > arr[i - 1] && arr[i] < arr[i + 1]) {
            return arr[i];
        }
    }
    return arr[0]; // If no pivot element is found, the first element is the minimum
}
```
This algorithm has a time complexity of O(log n) and uses O(1) extra space.

