```java
public class Main {
  public static void main(String[] args) {
    int num = 10;

    if (num % 1 = 0)
      System.out.println(num + "is an odd number.");
    else
      System.out.println(num + "is an even number.");
  }
}
```

response:
The code has two errors:

1. The conditional statement `if (num % 1 = 0)` is comparing the result of `num % 1` with `0`, but `num % 1` always returns `1` since `1` is a non-zero number and it will always be unequal to `0`. Therefore, this condition will always evaluate to false and the program will always print "10 is an odd number".
2. The comparison operator in Java is `==`, not `=`. In the code, the condition `if (num % 1 = 0)` should be `if (num % 1 == 0)`. This will fix the second error and make the program work as expected.

Here is a corrected version of the code:
```java
public class Main {
  public static void main(String[] args) {
    int num = 10;

    if (num % 2 == 0)
      System.out.println(num + "is an even number.");
    else
      System.out.println(num + "is an odd number.");
  }
}
```

