Here is a sample algorithm for sorting an array of integers using a simple bubble sort:
```
function bubbleSort(arr) {
  // Sort the array in ascending order
  for (let i = 0; i < arr.length - 1; i++) {
    for (let j = 0; j < arr.length - i - 1; j++) {
      if (arr[j] > arr[j + 1]) {
        // Swap the two elements if they are in the wrong order
        const temp = arr[j];
        arr[j] = arr[j + 1];
        arr[j + 1] = temp;
      }
    }
  }
  return arr;
}
```
This function takes an array of integers as input, and sorts it in ascending order using a bubble sort algorithm. The `for` loop iterates through the array, starting from the first element, and compares each element with its neighbor to see if they are in the wrong order. If they are, the two elements are swapped using the `temp` variable. At the end of the iteration, the smallest element is placed at the beginning of the array, so it can be compared with its neighbors on the next iteration. The process continues until all elements have been sorted.

Here's an example usage of the function:
```
const arr = [1, 5, 8, 2, 6, 3];
bubbleSort(arr);
console.log(arr); // Output: [1, 2, 3, 5, 6, 8]
```
This will sort the array in ascending order and print the sorted array to the console.

