```
import java.util.Scanner;

public class HighestNumber {
  public static void main(String[] args) {
    Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);

    System.out.print("Enter the numbers (separated by spaces): ");
    String input = sc.nextLine();

    int highestNum = Integer.MIN_VALUE;
    int highestCount = 0;

    // Check if the array is empty
    if (input.isEmpty()) {
      System.out.println("The array is empty.");
      return;
    }

    // Split the input string into an array of numbers
    String[] nums = input.split(" ");

    for (String num : nums) {
      int currentNum = Integer.parseInt(num);

      // Update the highest number and its count if necessary
      if (currentNum > highestNum) {
        highestNum = currentNum;
        highestCount = 1;
      } else if (currentNum == highestNum) {
        highestCount++;
      }
    }

    System.out.println("Highest number: " + highestNum);
    System.out.println("Number of times the highest number appears in the array: " + highestCount);
  }
}
```
This program first prompts the user to enter a list of numbers separated by spaces. It then splits this string into an array of numbers using the `split()` method and loops through each number in the array. For each number, it checks if it is higher than the current highest number, and if so, updates the highest number and its count accordingly. If two or more numbers are equal to the highest number, it increments the count of those numbers. Finally, it prints the highest number and its count to the console.

