The problem is that the generator has already been exhausted, and there are no more elements left to be yielded by the `next()` function. This means that the `emirp_root` variable is being assigned the value `None`, which is not a valid emirp root.

To fix this error, you can add an `if` statement before the `next()` call to check if there are any more elements in the generator. If there are no more elements, then you can break out of the loop and return a message indicating that the number is not an emirp. Here's an example:
```python
def is_emirp(n):
    if n <= 0:
        return "Invalid input"
    elif n == 1:
        return "The number 1 is not an emirp."
    else:
        for i in range(2, int(n**0.5) + 1):
            if n % i == 0:
                break
        else:
            # No more elements in the generator, so the number is not an emirp
            return f"{n} is not an emirp number."
        emirp_root = next(i for i in range(2, int(n**0.5) + 1) if n % i == 0)
        return f"{n} is an emirp number. The emirp root is {emirp_root}."
```
This will check if there are any more elements in the generator before calling `next()`, and if there are none, it will return a message indicating that the number is not an emirp.

Sure, here's an updated version of the code that takes into account larger matrices and includes functionality for non-square matrices:
```scss
function matrixMultiplication(matrix1, matrix2) {
  // Check if the matrices are valid
  if (!isValidMatrix(matrix1) || !isValidMatrix(matrix2)) {
    return null;
  }

  // Get the dimensions of the matrices
  const [rows1, cols1] = getDimensions(matrix1);
  const [rows2, cols2] = getDimensions(matrix2);

  // Check if the matrices are compatible for multiplication
  if (cols1 !== rows2) {
    return null;
  }

  // Initialize the result matrix
  const result = new Array(rows1);
  for (let i = 0; i < rows1; i++) {
    result[i] = new Array(cols2);
    for (let j = 0; j < cols2; j++) {
      result[i][j] = 0;
    }
  }

  // Perform the matrix multiplication
  for (let i = 0; i < rows1; i++) {
    for (let j = 0; j < cols2; j++) {
      for (let k = 0; k < cols1; k++) {
        result[i][j] += matrix1[i][k] * matrix2[k][j];
      }
    }
  }

  return result;
}

function isValidMatrix(matrix) {
  // Check if the matrix is a valid object
  if (!Array.isArray(matrix)) {
    return false;
  }

  // Check if the matrix has at least two rows and columns
  if (matrix.length < 2 || matrix[0].length < 2) {
    return false;
  }

  // Check if all elements in the matrix are numbers
  for (let i = 0; i < matrix.length; i++) {
    for (let j = 0; j < matrix[i].length; j++) {
      if (typeof matrix[i][j] !== 'number') {
        return false;
      }
    }
  }

  return true;
}

function getDimensions(matrix) {
  // Get the number of rows and columns in the matrix
  const rows = matrix.length;
  let cols = 0;
  if (rows > 0) {
    cols = matrix[0].length;
  }

  return [rows, cols];
}
```
This version of the code includes a function called `isValidMatrix` that checks whether a given object is a valid matrix. If it's not a valid matrix, the function returns `false`. The `getDimensions` function gets the number of rows and columns in a matrix and returns them as an array `[rows, cols]`.

The `matrixMultiplication` function now takes into account larger matrices by using the `isValidMatrix` and `getDimensions` functions to check if the matrices are valid and get their dimensions. If the matrices are not compatible for multiplication, the function returns `null`. Otherwise, it performs the matrix multiplication and returns the result.

Note that this version of the code also includes functionality for non-square matrices by checking if the number of rows and columns in the second matrix is equal to the number of columns in the first matrix. If they are not equal, the function returns `null`.

Here's an example implementation of the Contact Book class hierarchy:
```python
class ContactBook:
    def __init__(self):
        self.groups = {}  # Dictionary to store group objects

    def add_group(self, name):
        """Add a new group with the given name."""
        self.groups[name] = Group()

    def remove_group(self, name):
        """Remove the group with the given name."""
        del self.groups[name]

    def get_group(self, name):
        """Return the group with the given name."""
        return self.groups[name]

class Group:
    def __init__(self):
        self.contacts = []  # List to store contact objects

    def add_contact(self, contact):
        """Add a new contact to the group."""
        self.contacts.append(contact)

    def remove_contact(self, contact):
        """Remove the given contact from the group."""
        self.contacts.remove(contact)

    def get_contacts(self):
        """Return a list of all contacts in the group."""
        return self.contacts

class Contact:
    def __init__(self, name, phone_number, email_address):
        self.name = name
        self.phone_number = phone_number
        self.email_address = email_address

    def set_name(self, name):
        """Set the name of the contact."""
        self.name = name

    def get_name(self):
        """Get the name of the contact."""
        return self.name

    def set_phone_number(self, phone_number):
        """Set the phone number of the contact."""
        self.phone_number = phone_number

    def get_phone_number(self):
        """Get the phone number of the contact."""
        return self.phone_number

    def set_email_address(self, email_address):
        """Set the email address of the contact."""
        self.email_address = email_address

    def get_email_address(self):
        """Get the email address of the contact."""
        return self.email_address

class PersonalContact(Contact):
    def __init__(self, name, phone_number, email_address, relationship):
        super().__init__(name, phone_number, email_address)
        self.relationship = relationship

    def set_relationship(self, relationship):
        """Set the relationship of the contact."""
        self.relationship = relationship

    def get_relationship(self):
        """Get the relationship of the contact."""
        return self.relationship

class BusinessContact(Contact):
    def __init__(self, name, phone_number, email_address, organisation, job_title):
        super().__init__(name, phone_number, email_address)
        self.organisation = organisation
        self.job_title = job_title

    def set_organisation(self, organisation):
        """Set the organisation of the contact."""
        self.organisation = organisation

    def get_organisation(self):
        """Get the organisation of the contact."""
        return self.organisation

    def set_job_title(self, job_title):
        """Set the job title of the contact."""
        self.job_title = job_title

    def get_job_title(self):
        """Get the job title of the contact."""
        return self.job_title
```
