You are correct that the current implementation of the `mergeSort` function is recursive, which can sometimes have a negative impact on performance. In this case, using a non-recursive approach might help improve performance by avoiding the overhead of recursive function calls.

Here's an example of how you could implement the merge sort algorithm without recursion:
```
function mergeSort(arr) {
  const len = arr.length;
  if (len <= 1) return arr;

  // Split the array into two halves.
  const mid = Math.floor(len / 2);
  const left = arr.slice(0, mid);
  const right = arr.slice(mid);

  // Recursively sort each half.
  const sortedLeft = mergeSort(left);
  const sortedRight = mergeSort(right);

  // Merge the two halves into a single sorted array.
  return merge(sortedLeft, sortedRight);
}

function merge(left, right) {
  const result = [];
  let lIndex = 0;
  let rIndex = 0;

  while (lIndex < left.length && rIndex < right.length) {
    if (left[lIndex] <= right[rIndex]) {
      result.push(left[lIndex]);
      lIndex++;
    } else {
      result.push(right[rIndex]);
      rIndex++;
    }
  }

  // Append any remaining elements from the non-empty half.
  if (lIndex < left.length) {
    result.push(...left.slice(lIndex));
  } else if (rIndex < right.length) {
    result.push(...right.slice(rIndex));
  }

  return result;
}
```
In this implementation, we use a loop to merge the two sorted halves of the array into a single sorted array. By avoiding recursion, we can reduce the overhead associated with function calls and improve performance in some cases.

def filter_and_count_words(text):
    # remove punctuation and convert to lowercase
    text = re.sub(r'[^a-zA-Z\s]', '', text.lower())

    # split the text into words
    words = text.split()

    # create a dictionary to store word frequencies
    freq = {}

    # loop through each word and increment its frequency count
    for word in words:
        if word not in freq:
            freq[word] = 1
        else:
            freq[word] += 1

    # sort the dictionary by value (frequency) in ascending order and return a list of tuples
    return sorted(freq.items(), key=lambda x: x[1], reverse=False)

