```
void selectionSort(int arr[], int n) {
    // Initialize the minimum element and its index
    int min_idx, min;

    for (int i = 0; i < n - 1; i++) {
        // Set the minimum element to the first element of the unsorted part
        min = arr[i];
        min_idx = i;

        // Find the smallest element in the unsorted part
        for (int j = i + 1; j < n; j++) {
            if (arr[j] < min) {
                min = arr[j];
                min_idx = j;
            }
        }

        // Swap the minimum element with the first element of the unsorted part
        int temp = arr[i];
        arr[i] = arr[min_idx];
        arr[min_idx] = temp;
    }
}
```
In this version, we have added comments to explain the logic behind choosing the swap candidates.

The first for loop iterates over each element in the array that has not been sorted. For each iteration, it sets the minimum element and its index to the current element and its index.

The second for loop iterates over the unsorted part of the array starting from the next element after the current element. It compares each element in the unsorted part with the current minimum element and updates the minimum element and its index if a smaller element is found.

Finally, it swaps the current minimum element with the first element of the unsorted part using a temporary variable to avoid overwriting the original array.

By adding these comments, the code becomes more readable and easier to understand.

