Here is an example of how you can create a doubly linked list in C++ with both forward and backward pointers:
```
#include <iostream>

struct Node {
  int data;
  Node* next;
  Node* prev;
};

void printList(Node* head) {
  std::cout << "Forward traversal: ";
  while (head != nullptr) {
    std::cout << head->data << " ";
    head = head->next;
  }
  std::cout << std::endl;

  std::cout << "Backward traversal: ";
  while (head != nullptr) {
    std::cout << head->data << " ";
    head = head->prev;
  }
  std::cout << std::endl;
}

int main() {
  Node* head = nullptr;
  Node* tail = nullptr;

  // Create a list with 5 nodes
  for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
    Node* newNode = new Node();
    newNode->data = i + 1;
    newNode->next = head;
    if (head != nullptr) {
      head->prev = newNode;
    }
    head = newNode;
    tail = newNode;
  }

  printList(head);

  // Delete the list
  while (head != nullptr) {
    Node* temp = head;
    head = head->next;
    delete temp;
  }

  return 0;
}
```
This program creates a doubly linked list with 5 nodes, where each node contains both a forward and backward pointer. The list is traversed in both the forward and backward direction using two separate loops. Finally, the list is deleted by traversing it backwards and deleting each node as it is encountered.

To optimize the loop for large strings, we can use a faster algorithm that has a time complexity of O(n), where n is the length of the string. One such algorithm is the Knuth-Morris-Pratt (KMP) algorithm.

The KMP algorithm works by first finding all occurrences of the pattern in the string and then checking if any of those occurrences are followed by a character that is not in the pattern. If there is a match, we remove the last occurrence of the pattern from the string and repeat the process until there are no more matches.

Here is an example implementation of the KMP algorithm in Python:
```
def kmp(pattern, text):
    # Find all occurrences of the pattern in the text
    occurrences = []
    for i in range(len(text)):
        if text[i:].startswith(pattern):
            occurrences.append(i)

    # Check if any of the occurrences are followed by a character that is not in the pattern
    for i in range(len(occurrences)):
        if text[occurrences[i] + len(pattern)] not in pattern:
            return False

    # If there are no matches, return True
    return True
```
This implementation uses a list to store all occurrences of the pattern in the text, and then iterates over that list to check if any of the occurrences are followed by a character that is not in the pattern. If there are no matches, it returns True.

To make this more scalable, we can use a data structure such as a hash table or a trie to store all occurrences of the pattern in the text. This would allow us to quickly check if any of the occurrences are followed by a character that is not in the pattern, without having to iterate over the entire string.

Here is an example implementation of the KMP algorithm using a hash table:
```
def kmp_hash(pattern, text):
    # Create a hash table to store all occurrences of the pattern in the text
    occurrences = {}
    for i in range(len(text)):
        if text[i:].startswith(pattern):
            occurrences[i] = True

    # Check if any of the occurrences are followed by a character that is not in the pattern
    for i in range(len(occurrences)):
        if text[occurrences.keys()[i] + len(pattern)] not in pattern:
            return False

    # If there are no matches, return True
    return True
```
This implementation uses a hash table to store all occurrences of the pattern in the text, and then iterates over that hash table to check if any of the occurrences are followed by a character that is not in the pattern. If there are no matches, it returns True.

Overall, using a faster algorithm like the KMP algorithm can significantly improve the performance of the loop for large strings. However, it's important to note that the trade-off is that the algorithm may be more complex and harder to understand, which can make it less scalable in certain cases.

It's important to include comments in your code to make it more understandable and maintainable. Comments are notes or descriptions that explain why a particular piece of code is written the way it is. They can help other developers understand what the code does, how it works, and how it can be improved.

