Let's say we want to calculate the area of a rectangle with a length `l` and a width `w`. We can write a Java program that uses classes and methods to perform these calculations. Here is an example:
```
public class Rectangle {
    private double length;
    private double width;

    public Rectangle(double length, double width) {
        this.length = length;
        this.width = width;
    }

    public double getArea() {
        return length * width;
    }
}
```
In this example, we have defined a class called `Rectangle` with two fields: `length` and `width`. The constructor takes the values for these fields as parameters and initializes them. We also define a method called `getArea()` that returns the area of the rectangle.

To use this class to calculate the area of a rectangle, we can create an instance of the class and call the `getArea()` method:
```
Rectangle rect = new Rectangle(5, 3);
double area = rect.getArea();
System.out.println("The area of the rectangle is " + area);
```
This will output "The area of the rectangle is 15".

Note that we have used encapsulation in this example by making the fields `length` and `width` private and providing access to them through the getter method `getArea()`. This ensures that only the methods within the class can modify the state of the object, which helps to maintain data integrity and prevent errors.

To create a Vue.js component that graphically represents a pentagonal arrangement with a configuration of six layers and ten nodes per layer, you can use the following steps:

1. Create a new Vue.js project using the Vue CLI or any other method of your choice.
2. Install the necessary dependencies for creating a 3D visualization in your project, such as Three.js, OBJLoader, and GLTFLoader.
3. Define the structure of your component, including the properties that will be used to represent the nodes and edges of the pentagonal arrangement.
4. Use the Three.js library to create a 3D scene and add the necessary elements, such as meshes for the nodes and edges, using the OBJLoader and GLTFLoader libraries to load the objects from external files.
5. Implement the functionality that allows users to interact with the 3D visualization, such as zooming, panning, and rotating, using the Three.js library's built-in tools or third-party plugins.
6. Style the component using CSS to make it visually appealing and responsive on different devices.
7. Test the component in your project and make any necessary adjustments to ensure that it works as expected.

Here is an example of how you could implement this in Vue.js:
```
<template>
  <div class="pentagonal-arrangement">
    <scene v-for="layer in layers" :key="layer">
      <!-- nodes -->
      <mesh
        v-for="node in layer"
        :key="node.id"
        :position="node.position"
        :scale="node.scale"
        :rotation="node.rotation"
        material="OBJLoader(node.obj)"
      ></mesh>
      <!-- edges -->
      <line v-for="edge in layer" :key="edge.id">
        <geometry :position="edge.start" :end="edge.end"></geometry>
      </line>
    </scene>
  </div>
</template>

<script>
export default {
  name: 'PentagonalArrangement',
  props: {
    layers: Array,
  },
  data() {
    return {
      nodes: [],
      edges: [],
    };
  },
  mounted() {
    // load objects from external files using OBJLoader and GLTFLoader
    this.nodes = [...];
    this.edges = [...];
  },
  methods: {
    zoom(delta) {
      const camera = this.$refs.camera;
      camera.position.zoom += delta;
    },
    pan(delta) {
      const camera = this.$refs.camera;
      camera.position.x -= delta.x;
      camera.position.y -= delta.y;
    },
    rotate(angle) {
      const camera = this.$refs.camera;
      camera.rotation.y += angle;
    },
  },
};
</script>

<style scoped>
.pentagonal-arrangement {
  position: relative;
}

.scene {
  position: absolute;
  top: 0;
  left: 0;
  width: 100%;
  height: 100%;
}
</style>
```
In this example, the `PentagonalArrangement` component uses the Three.js library to create a 3D scene with nodes and edges that are represented by meshes and lines, respectively. The component also includes methods for zooming, panning, and rotating the camera, as well as CSS styles for making the visualization responsive on different devices.

To use this component in your Vue.js project, you can simply import it into a new Vue file and pass an array of objects representing the layers of the pentagonal arrangement to the `layers` prop. For example:
```
