public class PascalTriangle {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int n = 5; // Read input from user
        printPascalTriangle(n);
    }

    private static void printPascalTriangle(int n) {
        for (int row = 0; row < n; row++) {
            // Print header
            System.out.print("   ");
            for (int col = 0; col <= row; col++) {
                System.out.printf("%4d", 1);
            }
            System.out.println();

            // Print body
            for (int rowIndex = 1; rowIndex < n - 1; rowIndex++) {
                System.out.print("   ");
                for (int col = 0; col <= row; col++) {
                    int num = getPascalNumber(row, col);
                    System.out.printf("%4d", num);
                }
                System.out.println();
            }
        }
    }

    private static int getPascalNumber(int row, int col) {
        if (col == 0 || col == row) {
            return 1;
        } else {
            return getPascalNumber(row - 1, col - 1) + getPascalNumber(row - 1, col);
        }
    }
}
```
This code will print the Pascal Triangle with n rows. The header row has all 1s, and the body rows have the binomial coefficients as described in the problem statement.

Here is a Java program that calculates the products and prime counts of the major diagonal, minor diagonal, central row, and central column of a square matrix:
```
import java.util.Scanner;

public class MatrixDiagonals {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);

        System.out.print("Enter the number of rows and columns for the matrix: ");
        int n = sc.nextInt();

        // Ensure that the number of rows is odd
        if (n % 2 == 0) {
            n++;
        }

        int[][] matrix = new int[n][n];

        System.out.println("Enter the elements of the matrix:");
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            for (int j = 0; j < n; j++) {
                matrix[i][j] = sc.nextInt();
            }
        }

        // Calculate the products and prime counts of the major diagonal, minor diagonal, central row, and central column
        int majorDiagonalProduct = 1;
        int majorDiagonalPrimeCount = 0;
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            majorDiagonalProduct *= matrix[i][i];
            if (isPrime(matrix[i][i])) {
                majorDiagonalPrimeCount++;
            }
        }

        int minorDiagonalProduct = 1;
        int minorDiagonalPrimeCount = 0;
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            minorDiagonalProduct *= matrix[i][n - i - 1];
            if (isPrime(matrix[i][n - i - 1])) {
                minorDiagonalPrimeCount++;
            }
        }

        int centralRowProduct = 1;
        int centralRowPrimeCount = 0;
        for (int j = 0; j < n; j++) {
            centralRowProduct *= matrix[n / 2][j];
            if (isPrime(matrix[n / 2][j])) {
                centralRowPrimeCount++;
            }
        }

        int centralColumnProduct = 1;
        int centralColumnPrimeCount = 0;
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            centralColumnProduct *= matrix[i][n / 2];
            if (isPrime(matrix[i][n / 2])) {
                centralColumnPrimeCount++;
            }
        }

        System.out.println("Major Diagonal Product: " + majorDiagonalProduct);
        System.out.println("Major Diagonal Prime Count: " + majorDiagonalPrimeCount);
        System.out.println("Minor Diagonal Product: " + minorDiagonalProduct);
        System.out.println("Minor Diagonal Prime Count: " + minorDiagonalPrimeCount);
        System.out.println("Central Row Product: " + centralRowProduct);
        System.out.println("Central Row Prime Count: " + centralRowPrimeCount);
        System.out.println("Central Column Product: " + centralColumnProduct);
        System.out.println("Central Column Prime Count: " + centralColumnPrimeCount);
    }

    // Check if a number is prime
    public static boolean isPrime(int n) {
        if (n <= 1) {
            return false;
        }
        for (int i = 2; i * i <= n; i++) {
            if (n % i == 0) {
                return false;
            }
        }
        return true;
    }
}
```
This program first prompts the user to enter the number of rows and columns for the matrix. It then creates a two-dimensional array with the specified number of rows and columns, and prompts the user to enter the elements of the matrix.

Next, the program calculates the products and prime counts of the major diagonal, minor diagonal, central row, and central column of the matrix using nested loops. It uses the `isPrime` method to check if a number is prime. If a number is prime, it is counted as a prime number in the corresponding product and prime count.

Finally, the program prints out the products and prime counts of the major diagonal, minor diagonal, central row, and central column.

Note that this program assumes that the input matrix has an odd number of rows and columns, to ensure there is a central row and column. If the user enters an even number of rows or columns, the program will automatically increase the number by 1 to ensure there is a central row and column.

