This problem can be solved using a combination of Dijkstra's algorithm and the concept of memoization. The idea is to use Dijkstra's algorithm to find the shortest path from index 1 to index n, while also keeping track of the minimum cost for each node in the graph. We can use a hash table to store the minimum cost for each node, which allows us to avoid revisiting nodes that we have already visited with a lower cost.

Here is an example implementation:
```
class Solution {
    public int[] shortestPath(int[] coins, int maxJump) {
        // Create a graph where each node represents a position in the array
        Graph graph = new Graph(coins);

        // Initialize the minimum cost for each node to infinity
        int[] minCost = new int[graph.nodes];
        Arrays.fill(minCost, Integer.MAX_VALUE);

        // Set the minimum cost for the starting position to 0
        minCost[1] = 0;

        // Use Dijkstra's algorithm to find the shortest path from the starting position
        PriorityQueue<Node> queue = new PriorityQueue<>();
        queue.offer(new Node(1, 0));
        while (!queue.isEmpty()) {
            Node current = queue.poll();
            for (int neighbor : graph.neighbors[current.index]) {
                int cost = Math.min(current.cost + coins[neighbor], minCost[neighbor]);
                if (cost < minCost[neighbor]) {
                    minCost[neighbor] = cost;
                    queue.offer(new Node(neighbor, cost));
                }
            }
        }

        // Find the path that reaches the end position with the minimum cost
        int endPosition = graph.nodes - 1;
        int currentPosition = endPosition;
        List<Integer> path = new ArrayList<>();
        while (currentPosition != 0) {
            path.add(currentPosition);
            currentPosition = minCost[currentPosition] == Integer.MAX_VALUE ? 0 : currentPosition - 1;
        }
        Collections.reverse(path);

        return path.stream().mapToInt(i -> i).toArray();
    }
}

class Graph {
    int[] nodes;
    List<List<Integer>> neighbors;

    public Graph(int[] coins) {
        this.nodes = new int[coins.length];
        this.neighbors = new ArrayList<>();

        for (int i = 0; i < nodes.length; i++) {
            neighbors.add(new ArrayList<>());
            if (i + 1 <= coins.length) {
                int cost = coins[i + 1];
                if (cost != -1 && i + cost <= coins.length) {
                    neighbors.get(i).add(i + cost);
                }
            }
            if (i - 1 >= 0) {
                int cost = coins[i - 1];
                if (cost != -1 && i - cost >= 0) {
                    neighbors.get(i).add(i - cost);
                }
            }
        }
    }
}

class Node implements Comparable<Node> {
    int index;
    int cost;

    public Node(int index, int cost) {
        this.index = index;
        this.cost = cost;
    }

    @Override
    public int compareTo(Node o) {
        return Integer.compare(this.cost, o.cost);
    }
}
```
This solution has a time complexity of O(n\*m), where n is the length of the input array and m is the maximum jump distance. The space complexity is O(n\*m) as well, since we need to store the minimum cost for each node in the graph.

