To convert X and Y coordinates into longitude and latitude using a geoid-corrected method, we can use the following formula:

Latitude (φ) = arctan(X / (Y - 100))
Longitude (λ) = arctan((Y - 100) / X)

Where 100 is a constant that adjusts for the curvature of the Earth and the geoid shape.

Here's an example implementation in JavaScript:
```javascript
function convertCoordinates(X, Y) {
  const PI = Math.PI;
  const CENTER_LONGITUDE = -180; // Adjust this value for your specific geoid model
  const CENTER_LATITUDE = 90; // Adjust this value for your specific geoid model

  let latitude = Math.atan2(X, Y - 100) * PI / 180;
  let longitude = Math.atan2((Y - 100), X) * PI / 180 + CENTER_LONGITUDE;

  return [longitude, latitude];
}

let X = 83.206226;
