Here is an implementation of the Bellman-Ford algorithm in JavaScript:
```
function bellmanFord(graph, startVertex) {
  // Initialize the distance array with infinity
  const distances = new Array(graph.length).fill(Infinity);

  // Set the starting vertex to have a distance of 0
  distances[startVertex] = 0;

  // Loop until no more vertices can be relaxed
  for (let i = 0; i < graph.length; i++) {
    let relaxed = false;

    // Iterate over all edges in the graph
    for (const edge of graph) {
      const start = edge[0];
      const end = edge[1];

      // If the distance to the end vertex is greater than the sum of the distances
      // from the starting vertex and the weight of the edge, relax the distance
      if (distances[start] + graph.getEdgeWeight(start, end) < distances[end]) {
        distances[end] = distances[start] + graph.getEdgeWeight(start, end);
        relaxed = true;
      }
    }

    // If no vertices were relaxed during this iteration, the algorithm has converged
    if (!relaxed) break;
  }

  return distances;
}
```
Here's a step-by-step explanation of how the algorithm works:

1. The `bellmanFord` function takes in a graph and a starting vertex as input, and returns an array containing the shortest distance from the starting vertex to every other vertex in the graph.
2. The algorithm starts by initializing an empty array with the same length as the number of vertices in the graph. Each element in this array is initialized to `Infinity`. This will serve as the distance array for the algorithm, and will be used to store the shortest distance from the starting vertex to each other vertex in the graph.
3. The next line sets the starting vertex to have a distance of 0. This means that we can reach the starting vertex directly from itself with no edges.
4. The algorithm then enters a loop where it will continue to relax distances until all vertices have been reached. During each iteration, the `relaxed` variable is set to false. If any vertex is successfully relaxed during this iteration, the `relaxed` variable is set to true.
5. Inside the main loop, we iterate over every edge in the graph and check if it can be used to relax the distance to a neighboring vertex. We do this by first retrieving the starting and ending vertices of the current edge from the graph using the `edge[0]` and `edge[1]` properties.
6. If the distance to the ending vertex is greater than the sum of the distances from the starting vertex and the weight of the edge, we know that we can relax the distance to this neighboring vertex by setting its distance equal to the sum of these two values minus the weight of the edge. We set `relaxed` to true in this case, as at least one vertex has been successfully relaxed during this iteration.
7. If no vertices were relaxed during this iteration, we know that all vertices have been reached and the algorithm can be terminated. We break out of the loop and return the distance array.
8. The final line of the `bellmanFord` function returns the distance array containing the shortest distance from the starting vertex to every other vertex in the graph.

Overall, this implementation of the Bellman-Ford algorithm uses a distance array to store the shortest distance from the starting vertex to each other vertex in the graph. It then iteratively relaxes distances until all vertices have been reached, using a loop to check for relaxations and terminate when no more relaxations can be made.

