```
// Prime Checking
function isPrime(n) {
  if (n <= 1) return false; // Base case: n is not prime if it's less than or equal to 1
  for (let i = 2; i < n; i++) {
    if (n % i === 0) return false; // If n is divisible by any number between 2 and n-1, it's not prime
  }
  return true; // If we reach this point, n is prime
}

// Factorial Calculation
function factorial(n) {
  if (n === 0 || n === 1) return 1; // Base cases: n! = 1 for n = 0 or n = 1
  return n * factorial(n - 1); // Recursive case: compute n! as the product of n and (n-1)!
}
```
In this example, I have added comments to explain what each function does. The `isPrime` function checks if a given number is prime by iterating from 2 to the square root of the number and checking if it is divisible. If the number is not divisible by any number between 2 and the square root of the number, it is prime.

The `factorial` function computes the factorial of a given number using a recursive approach. The base cases are when the number is 0 or 1, in which case the factorial is defined to be 1. Otherwise, the factorial is computed as the product of the number and the factorial of the number minus 1.

