To perform client-side validation, we can add JavaScript code that validates the inputs when the user submits the form. For example:
```javascript
// Get references to the input fields
const numberInput = document.getElementById('number');
const operatorSelect = document.getElementById('operator');

// Define functions for validation
function validateNumber() {
  // Check if the input value is a positive integer
  const num = parseInt(numberInput.value);
  if (isNaN(num) || num < 1) {
    alert('Please enter a positive integer for the number.');
    return false;
  } else {
    return true;
  }
}

function validateOperator() {
  // Check if the operator is one of the allowed options
  const operator = operatorSelect.value;
  if (operator !== '+' && operator !== '-' && operator !== '*' && operator !== '/') {
    alert('Please select a valid operator from the list.');
    return false;
  } else {
    return true;
  }
}

// Add event listener to form submission
document.getElementById('arithmetic-operations').addEventListener('submit', function(event) {
  // Prevent default form submission behavior
  event.preventDefault();

  // Validate the inputs
  if (!validateNumber() || !validateOperator()) {
    return;
  }

  // Calculate the result of the arithmetic operation
  const num = parseInt(numberInput.value);
  const operator = operatorSelect.value;
  let result = '';
  switch (operator) {
    case '+':
      result = num + num;
      break;
    case '-':
      result = num - num;
      break;
    case '*':
      result = num * num;
      break;
    case '/':
      result = num / num;
      break;
  }

  // Display the result
  document.getElementById('result').innerHTML = `The result is ${result}`;
});
```
In this code, we first define two functions for validating the input values: `validateNumber()` and `validateOperator()`. These functions check if the input values are positive integers and if the operator selected is one of the allowed options. If either validation fails, an error message is displayed using `alert()`.

We then add an event listener to the form's `submit` event to validate the inputs before performing any calculations. We use `event.preventDefault()` to prevent the default form submission behavior and manually submit the form data. If either input value or operator are invalid, we return without submitting the form.

Once both inputs are validated, we perform the arithmetic operation based on the selected operator using the `switch` statement. Finally, we display the result of the calculation in an element with the ID `result`.

