It sounds like you are experiencing a cross-origin resource sharing (CORS) issue. This occurs when your frontend and backend servers are hosted on different domains, and your browser is blocking the request because of security concerns. To fix this issue, you can enable CORS on your backend server by adding the necessary headers to your responses.

Here's an example of how you can do this in Node.js:
```
app.use(function (req, res, next) {
  res.setHeader('Access-Control-Allow-Origin', '*');
  res.setHeader('Access-Control-Allow-Methods', 'GET, POST, PUT, DELETE');
  res.setHeader('Access-Control-Allow-Headers', 'X-Requested-With,content-type');
  res.setHeader('Access-Control-Allow-Credentials', true);
  next();
});
```
This code enables CORS for all routes in your backend server by setting the `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` header to `'*'`. This allows any domain to make requests to your backend server, regardless of their origin. You can also specify specific origins if you want to limit access further.

You can also set the `Access-Control-Allow-Credentials` header to `true` to allow cookies and other authentication credentials to be sent with CORS requests.

Once you've enabled CORS on your backend server, you should see your frontend requests start working again.

