Here is a Swift function that finds the maximum number in an array of integers and returns its index:
```swift
func findMaxIndex(arr: [Int]) -> (maxValue: Int, maxIndex: Int) {
    guard arr.count > 0 else { return (0, -1) }

    // Base case: if the array has only one element, return it as both the maximum value and its index
    if arr.count == 1 {
        return (arr[0], 0)
    }

    // Recursive case: find the maximum value in the left half of the array
    let (leftMaxValue, leftMaxIndex) = findMaxIndex(Array(arr[0..<(arr.count/2)]))

    // Recursive case: find the maximum value in the right half of the array
    let (rightMaxValue, rightMaxIndex) = findMaxIndex(Array(arr[(arr.count/2)..<arr.count]))

    // Return the maximum value and its index from the left and right halves
    if leftMaxValue > rightMaxValue {
        return (leftMaxValue, leftMaxIndex + arr.count/2)
    } else {
        return (rightMaxValue, rightMaxIndex + arr.count/2)
    }
}
```
To use this function, you can pass an array of integers to it and it will return the maximum value in the array and its index:
```swift
let arr = [10, 5, 3, 8, 20, 15]
let (maxValue, maxIndex) = findMaxIndex(arr)
print("Maximum value: \(maxValue)") // prints "Maximum value: 20"
print("Maximum index: \(maxIndex)") // prints "Maximum index: 4"
```
Note that this function uses a recursive approach to find the maximum value in the array. It divides the array into two halves, finds the maximum values in each half, and then compares them to determine which one is greater. The function then returns the maximum value and its index as a tuple.

