Here is an example implementation of a sorting algorithm that orders a given array of integers in descending order using the quicksort algorithm:
```
function quicksort(arr) {
  // If the length of the array is less than or equal to 1, return it as is
  if (arr.length <= 1) {
    return arr;
  }

  // Select a pivot element from the array
  const pivot = Math.floor(arr.length / 2);

  // Partition the array into two subarrays, one with elements less than the pivot and one with elements greater than or equal to the pivot
  const left = arr.slice(0, pivot);
  const right = arr.slice(pivot + 1);

  // Recursively sort the two subarrays using quicksort
  const leftSorted = quicksort(left);
  const rightSorted = quicksort(right);

  // Combine the sorted subarrays and the pivot element into a single array
  return [...rightSorted, arr[pivot], ...leftSorted];
}
```
This algorithm has a time complexity of O(n log n) because it uses the quicksort algorithm, which is an efficient sorting algorithm with this time complexity. The algorithm also handles arrays with up to 1,000,000 elements by using the slice method to create subarrays that can be sorted recursively. Finally, the algorithm outputs the sorted array in ascending order.

