Here are some potential ways to optimize the use of mixins for media queries in CSS:

1. Use a single mixin for all media queries: Instead of defining separate mixins for each breakpoint, you can define a single mixin that takes a breakpoint as an argument and applies the appropriate styles based on the value of the argument. For example:
```css
@mixin media-query($breakpoint) {
  @media (min-width: $breakpoint) {
    // Apply styles here
  }
}
```
2. Use a map to store media queries and their associated styles: You can define a map of media queries and their associated styles, and then use a single mixin to apply the appropriate styles based on the current breakpoint. For example:
```css
$media-queries: (
  small: "max-width: 400px",
  medium: "min-width: 401px",
  large: "min-width: 769px"
);

@mixin media-query($breakpoint) {
  @media ($media-queries[$breakpoint]) {
    // Apply styles here
  }
}
```
3. Use a dynamic variable to set the media query breakpoint: Instead of hardcoding the breakpoint in the mixin, you can use a dynamic variable that gets updated based on the current screen size. For example:
```css
$breakpoint: "small"; // Set the default breakpoint

@mixin media-query($breakpoint) {
  @media (min-width: $breakpoint) {
    // Apply styles here
  }
}

@media (max-width: 400px) {
  $breakpoint = "small"; // Set the breakpoint for small screens
}

@media (min-width: 401px) and (max-width: 768px) {
  $breakpoint = "medium"; // Set the breakpoint for medium screens
}

@media (min-width: 769px) {
  $breakpoint = "large"; // Set the breakpoint for large screens
}
```
By using one of these techniques, you can reduce repetition in your CSS code and make it easier to maintain and update.

To optimize the code for handling multiple API calls more efficiently, we can use the `async` and `await` keywords in JavaScript to perform asynchronous requests. This allows us to make multiple requests at once and wait for them to complete before continuing with the rest of the program.
```
async function getBookInfo(isbn) {
  const response = await fetch(`https://api.example.com/books?isbn=${isbn}`);
  return response.json();
}
```
We can also use `Promise.all` to make multiple requests at once and wait for all of them to complete before resolving the promise.
```
function getBookInfo(isbns) {
  const promises = isbns.map(isbn => fetch(`https://api.example.com/books?isbn=${isbn}`));
  return Promise.all(promises);
}
```
To scale the code to handle a larger number of book queries, we can use a database or a distributed cache like Redis to store the information about each book. This will allow us to retrieve the information more quickly and reduce the number of API calls needed. We can also use pagination to limit the number of books that need to be queried at once.
```
function getBookInfo(isbns) {
  const pageSize = 10; // maximum number of books per page
  const pages = Math.ceil(isbns.length / pageSize);
  const results = [];

  for (let i = 0; i < pages; i++) {
    const isbnsPage = isbns.slice(i * pageSize, (i + 1) * pageSize);
    const response = await fetch(`https://api.example.com/books?isbn=${isbnsPage.join(',')}`);
    results.push(...response.json());
  }

  return results;
}
```
In this example, we first calculate the number of pages needed to retrieve all the book information based on the maximum number of books per page. Then, we use a `for` loop to iterate over each page and make an API call for that page. We store the response in an array called `results`, which will contain all the book information after all the requests have been completed.

We can also use a library like `promise-all-settled` to handle multiple promises at once, it allows us to handle multiple promises and get the results as soon as they are available, it's useful when we need to make multiple API calls and want to process them all at once.
```
const { promiseAllSettled } = require('promise-all-settled');

function getBookInfo(isbns) {
  const promises = isbns.map(isbn => fetch(`https://api.example.com/books?isbn=${isbn}`));
  return promiseAllSettled(promises);
}
```
In this example, we first map each ISBN to a promise that will be used to retrieve the book information for that ISBN. Then, we use `promise-all-settled` to handle all the promises at once and get the results as soon as they are available.

It's important to note that using a database or distributed cache can have performance benefits, but it also requires additional infrastructure and maintenance.

