That's correct! Using Fermat's Little Theorem can significantly simplify the code and make it more efficient. The modified code with this optimization is as follows:
```scss
$p = 3;
$x = 2;
$y = 5;

$step1 = pow($x, $p-1) % $p;
$step2 = pow($y, $p-1) % $p;

if ($step1 == $step2) {
    echo "The numbers are relatively prime.";
} else {
    echo "The numbers are not relatively prime.";
}
```
In this version of the code, we use Fermat's Little Theorem to calculate the values of `pow($x, $p-1) % $p` and `pow($y, $p-1) % $p` directly. This eliminates the need for the separate step 1 calculation and simplifies the code.

Note that this optimization is only possible because we know that $p$ is a prime number, so we can use Fermat's Little Theorem to simplify the calculations. If we didn't have this knowledge, then the original version of the code would still be necessary to calculate the values of `pow($x, $p-1) % $p` and `pow($y, $p-1) % $p`.

Here is a solution in JavaScript that meets your requirements:
```
function checkAnagrams(str1, str2) {
  // Remove all duplicates from string str1
  let uniqueChars = new Set();
  for (let i = 0; i < str1.length; i++) {
    if (!uniqueChars.has(str1[i])) {
      uniqueChars.add(str1[i]);
    }
  }
  let sortedUniqueChars = [...uniqueChars].sort();

  // Remove all duplicates from string str2
  uniqueChars = new Set();
  for (let i = 0; i < str2.length; i++) {
    if (!uniqueChars.has(str2[i])) {
      uniqueChars.add(str2[i]);
    }
  }
  let sortedUniqueChars2 = [...uniqueChars].sort();

  // Check if the two strings are anagrams
  return sortedUniqueChars.join("") === sortedUniqueChars2.join("");
}
```
Explanation:

* We use a `Set` data structure to keep track of all the unique characters in each string. This is more efficient than using an array, because it allows for fast lookups and does not allow duplicates.
* We sort the resulting arrays of unique characters, so that we can compare them later.
* Finally, we use the `join` method to convert the sorted arrays into strings, and then check if they are equal.

The time complexity of this program is O(n log n), where n is the length of the input strings. This is because we need to sort the arrays of unique characters, which has a time complexity of O(n log n). However, since we only perform one pass through each array and do not perform any nested loops, the overall time complexity is less than O(n^2).

