// The Sieve of Eratosthenes is an algorithm for finding all prime numbers up to a given number n. It works by creating a boolean array of length n+1 and iteratively marking as composite (false) any number that is not prime.

// The algorithm starts with an array where all elements are initialized to true, indicating that all numbers are considered prime until proven otherwise.
boolean[] isPrime = new boolean[n+1];
for (int i=2; i<=n; i++) {
    // If the current number is prime, mark all its multiples as composite (false)
    if (isPrime[i]) {
        for (int j=i*i; j<=n; j+=i) {
            isPrime[j] = false;
        }
    }
}
```
The `isPrime` array is initialized to `true`, indicating that all numbers are considered prime until proven otherwise. The outer loop iterates over the range of numbers from 2 to `n+1`. For each number, we check if it is prime by checking if the corresponding entry in the `isPrime` array is `true`. If it is, we mark its multiples as composite (i.e., set their entries in the `isPrime` array to `false`) using the inner loop.

The reason why this algorithm works is that once we have marked a number's multiples as composite, those numbers are no longer considered prime candidates. Therefore, when we come across a number that is not marked as composite (i.e., its entry in the `isPrime` array is `true`), we know that it must be prime.

The running time of this algorithm is O(n log log n) since the inner loop iterates over the multiples of each prime number, which are at most n/2 for any given number. Therefore, the overall running time of the algorithm is O(n^2).

