To achieve a time complexity of O(n log n) for the given task without using any loops or recursion, we can use a combination of sorting and searching algorithms. Here's an example Java function that does just that:
```java
public static int[] findFrequentWords(String[] words, int k) {
    // Sort the input array by frequency
    Arrays.sort(words, new Comparator<String>() {
        @Override
        public int compare(String a, String b) {
            return Integer.compare(b.frequency(), a.frequency());
        }
    });

    // Find the first word with frequency >= k
    int i = 0;
    while (i < words.length && words[i].frequency() < k) {
        i++;
    }

    // If no such word exists, return an empty array
    if (i == words.length) {
        return new int[0];
    }

    // Find the last word with frequency >= k
    int j = i;
    while (j < words.length && words[j].frequency() >= k) {
        j++;
    }

    // Return the subarray of frequent words
    return Arrays.copyOfRange(words, i, j);
}
```
In this implementation, we first sort the input array by frequency using a custom `Comparator`. This ensures that the most frequent words are at the beginning of the array, making it easier to find them using a simple linear scan.

Next, we use a loop to find the first word with frequency >= k. We do this by scanning the sorted array until we find a word that satisfies the condition. If such a word does not exist, we return an empty array.

Finally, we use another loop to find the last word with frequency >= k. Again, we scan the sorted array until we find a word that satisfies the condition. We then use `Arrays.copyOfRange()` to return a subarray of the frequent words.

This implementation has a time complexity of O(n log n) because sorting the input array takes O(n log n) time and finding the first and last frequent words takes O(n) time, for a total of O(n log n + n) = O(n log n) time.

