```java
import java.util.concurrent.locks.ReentrantLock;

public class Game {
    private boolean yourTurn = false;
    private ReentrantLock lock = new ReentrantLock();

    public void player1Move() {
        if (!yourTurn) {
            // Wait until it is player 1's turn
            lock.lock();
            try {
                while (yourTurn == false) {
                    // Wait for the lock to be released by the other thread
                    Thread.sleep(100);
                }
            } catch (InterruptedException e) {
                System.out.println("InterruptedException: " + e.getMessage());
            } finally {
                lock.unlock();
            }
        }

        // Make the move
        yourTurn = false;
    }

    public void player2Move() {
        if (!yourTurn) {
            // Wait until it is player 2's turn
            lock.lock();
            try {
                while (yourTurn == true) {
                    // Wait for the lock to be released by the other thread
                    Thread.sleep(100);
                }
            } catch (InterruptedException e) {
                System.out.println("InterruptedException: " + e.getMessage());
            } finally {
                lock.unlock();
            }
        }

        // Make the move
        yourTurn = true;
    }
}
```
In this implementation, we use a `ReentrantLock` to synchronize access to the `yourTurn` variable. The `player1Move()` and `player2Move()` methods both acquire the lock before checking the value of `yourTurn`. If it is not their turn, they will wait for the lock to be released by the other thread using `Thread.sleep()`. Once the lock is acquired, they will update the value of `yourTurn` and release the lock.

The main benefit of using a `ReentrantLock` is that it allows multiple threads to acquire the lock multiple times, but it only releases the lock once all of the threads that have acquired it have released it. This ensures that no other thread can interfere with the turn-based system.

The downside of using a `ReentrantLock` is that it can cause contention between threads if there are too many threads competing for the lock. In this case, we could consider using a more efficient synchronization mechanism such as a `Semaphore`.

