Here's one way to write such a recursive function in C++:
```
#include <iostream>

int evaluate(int n) {
    if (n <= 0) {
        std::cout << "Invalid input: n must be a positive integer.";
        return -1;
    }

    int product = 1;
    for (int i = n; i > 0; --i) {
        product *= i;
    }

    return product;
}
```
This function takes a single argument `n` of type `int`, and it returns the value of the given expression. If `n` is less than or equal to 0, the function will print an error message to the console and return -1. Otherwise, it will calculate the product of all numbers from `n` to 1 using a loop and return the result.

