Here's an updated version of the `cl_kernel_mgr` class that includes the necessary methods to register and retrieve kernel functions:
```c++
class cl_kernel_mgr {
public:
    // Constructor
    cl_kernel_mgr() {}

    // Destructor
    ~cl_kernel_mgr() {
        for (auto& it : kernelMap) {
            delete it.second;
        }
    }

    // Register a new kernel function with the given function name and binary name
    void registerKernel(const std::string& functionName, const std::string& binaryName) {
        // Check if the kernel already exists
        if (kernelMap.find(functionName) != kernelMap.end()) {
            throw std::runtime_error("Kernel " + functionName + " already registered");
        }

        // Create a new wrapper object for the kernel function
        cl_kernel_wrapper* wrapper = new cl_kernel_wrapper();
        wrapper->functionName = functionName;
        wrapper->binaryName = binaryName;

        // Register the kernel with the map
        kernelMap[functionName] = wrapper;
    }

    // Retrieve a registered kernel function by its name
    cl_kernel_wrapper* getKernel(const std::string& functionName) {
        auto it = kernelMap.find(functionName);
        if (it == kernelMap.end()) {
            throw std::runtime_error("No such kernel: " + functionName);
        }

        return it->second;
    }

private:
    // Map to store the registered kernels
    std::map<std::string, cl_kernel_wrapper*> kernelMap;
};
```
The `registerKernel` method checks if a kernel with the given function name already exists in the map and throws an exception if it does. If the kernel doesn't exist, it creates a new wrapper object for the kernel function, sets its name and binary name, and registers it with the map.

The `getKernel` method retrieves a registered kernel function by its name from the map. It checks if the kernel exists in the map and throws an exception if it doesn't. If the kernel does exist, it returns the wrapper object for the kernel.

Note that the destructor of the `cl_kernel_mgr` class deletes all the wrapper objects created for the registered kernels to avoid memory leaks.

