Here's an example of a basic data streaming program using the Apache Flink framework:
```
import org.apache.flink.api.common.functions.FlatMapFunction;
import org.apache.flink.api.java.tuple.Tuple2;
import org.apache.flink.streaming.api.datastream.DataStream;
import org.apache.flink.streaming.api.environment.StreamExecutionEnvironment;

public class StreamingJob {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        // Set up the streaming execution environment
        final StreamExecutionEnvironment env = StreamExecutionEnvironment.getExecutionEnvironment();

        // Read data from a source (e.g., Kafka, FileSystem, or other sources)
        DataStream<Tuple2<String, Integer>> inputData = env.readTextFile("/path/to/input/data");

        // Transform the data using a user-defined function
        DataStream<Tuple2<String, String>> outputData = inputData.flatMap(new FlatMapFunction<Tuple2<String, Integer>, Tuple2<String, String>>() {
            @Override
            public void flatMap(Tuple2<String, Integer> value, Collector<Tuple2<String, String>> out) throws Exception {
                out.collect(new Tuple2<>(value.f0, "Transformed value: " + value.f1));
            }
        });

        // Write the output data to a sink (e.g., Kafka, FileSystem, or other sinks)
        outputData.writeAsText("/path/to/output/data", FileSystem.WriteMode.OVERWRITE);

        // Execute the streaming job
        env.execute();
    }
}
```
This example reads text data from a file, transforms it using a user-defined `FlatMapFunction`, and then writes the transformed data to another file. The `StreamExecutionEnvironment` is used to set up the streaming execution environment, and the `DataStream` API is used to read and write data.

Note that this is just a simple example to demonstrate the basic concepts of Apache Flink. In a real-world scenario, you would need to handle errors, fault tolerance, and performance optimization.

