Here is an optimized version of the bubble sort algorithm in JavaScript that sorts in descending order:
```
function bubbleSort(arr) {
  let n = arr.length;
  for (let i = 0; i < n - 1; i++) {
    for (let j = 0; j < n - i - 1; j++) {
      if (arr[j] < arr[j + 1]) {
        let temp = arr[j];
        arr[j] = arr[j + 1];
        arr[j + 1] = temp;
      }
    }
  }
  return arr;
}
```
This algorithm works by iterating through the array, and swapping adjacent elements if they are in the wrong order. The outer loop iterates over all elements in the array, while the inner loop iterates over all elements after the first element. If a pair of adjacent elements is not in descending order, they are swapped.

This algorithm has a time complexity of O(n^2), which means that the time it takes to sort an array increases as the size of the array increases. However, this implementation is optimized by using a temporary variable to store the values being swapped, which reduces the number of writes to the array and makes the algorithm more efficient.

You can test this algorithm on the following array: [5, 2, 8, 3, 1, 6, 4]
