To parse the configuration file and extract key-value pairs, you can use the following implementation:
```java
public Map<String, String> parseConfigurationFile(String filePath) throws IOException {
    // Create a map to store the extracted key-value pairs
    Map<String, String> config = new HashMap<>();

    // Open the configuration file for reading
    try (BufferedReader reader = new BufferedReader(new FileReader(filePath))) {
        String line;

        // Iterate over each line in the file
        while ((line = reader.readLine()) != null) {
            // Ignore empty lines and lines starting with a hash symbol (#)
            if (!line.trim().isEmpty() && !line.startsWith("#")) {
                // Extract the key and value from the line
                String[] parts = line.split("=");
                String key = parts[0].trim();
                String value = parts[1].trim();

                // Add the extracted key-value pair to the map
                config.put(key, value);
            }
        }
    }

    return config;
}
```
This implementation uses a `BufferedReader` to read the lines of the configuration file one by one. It then iterates over each line and extracts the key-value pair using the `split()` method, which splits the line into an array of substrings based on the equal sign (`=`). The empty lines and lines starting with a hash symbol (#) are ignored, and only the valid key-value pairs are added to the map.

To test this implementation, you can use the following code:
```java
public static void main(String[] args) throws IOException {
    String filePath = "config.txt";
    Map<String, String> config = parseConfigurationFile(filePath);

    // Print the contents of the map
    System.out.println("Config:");
    for (Map.Entry<String, String> entry : config.entrySet()) {
        System.out.printf("%s=%s%n", entry.getKey(), entry.getValue());
    }
}
```
This code reads the configuration file using the `parseConfigurationFile()` method and prints its contents to the console.

