To optimize the function for performance, we can reduce the number of regular expression tests by using a more efficient algorithm. One such algorithm is the "Knuth-Morris-Pratt" (KMP) algorithm, which is a linear-time substring search algorithm that can be used to find all occurrences of a given pattern in a text.

Here's an example implementation of the KMP algorithm in JavaScript:
```javascript
function kmp(text, pattern) {
  let i = 0; // index of the current character in the text
  let j = 0; // index of the current character in the pattern
  while (i < text.length && j < pattern.length) {
    if (text[i] === pattern[j]) {
      i++;
      j++;
    } else if (j > 0) {
      j = 0; // reset the index of the current character in the pattern
      i++; // move to the next character in the text
    } else {
      i++; // move to the next character in the text
    }
  }
  return j === pattern.length; // return true if the pattern is found in the text
}
```
We can use this function to optimize the original implementation of the email validation function by using a more efficient algorithm for finding the "@". Here's an example:
```javascript
function validateEmail(email) {
  const pattern = /\w+@\w+\.\w+/; // regex pattern for validating emails
  let match = kmp(email, pattern); // use KMP algorithm to find all occurrences of the pattern in the email
  return match !== null && match.length > 0; // return true if the pattern is found and it's not an empty string
}
```
