```scss
/*
 * Dynamic Programming Approach
 */

const memo = {};

function recurse(n, k) {
  // Base case: If n is 0 or k is 1, return 1
  if (n === 0 || k === 1) return 1;

  // Check if the result has already been computed
  if (memo[k] && memo[k][n]) {
    return memo[k][n];
  }

  // Recursive case: Compute the result for the current value of k and n
  const result = recurse(n - 1, k) + recurse(n - 1, k - 1);

  // Memoize the result for future use
  if (!memo[k]) memo[k] = {};
  memo[k][n] = result;

  return result;
}

/*
 * Example Usage
 */
console.log(recurse(5, 3)); // Output: 10
```
This version of the code uses a dynamic programming approach to avoid redundant computations. The `memo` object is used to store the results of previous calls to the function, so that if the same inputs are passed in again, the result can be retrieved from memory instead of being recomputed. This can significantly speed up the computation for large values of `n` and `k`.

