To convert an integer to a 32-bit binary representation without using any built-in functions, we can use the following steps:

1. Check if the number is negative. If it is, negate it and store the result in a new variable. This will ensure that the most significant bit (MSb) of the binary representation will be 1 for negative numbers.
2. Divide the absolute value of the integer by 2 until we reach a value less than or equal to 0. The quotient of each division step will represent one digit in our binary representation.
3. Store the remainder from each division step as an array.
4. Create a new string with the same number of digits as the length of the remainder array. For each index, set the value at that position to be either 0 or 1 depending on whether the corresponding remainder is odd or even.
5. Return the binary representation as a string.

Here's an example implementation in JavaScript:
```
function convertToBinary(num) {
  let negative = false;
  if (num < 0) {
    negative = true;
    num = -num;
  }

  const quotient = [];
  let remainder = num;
  while (remainder > 0) {
    const div = Math.floor(remainder / 2);
    quotient.push(div);
    remainder -= div * 2;
  }

  const binaryRepresentation = [];
  for (let i = 0; i < quotient.length; i++) {
    if (quotient[i] % 2 === 1) {
      binaryRepresentation.push(1);
    } else {
      binaryRepresentation.push(0);
    }
  }

  return negative ? '-' + binaryRepresentation : binaryRepresentation;
}
```
This function takes an integer as input and returns its 32-bit binary representation without using any built-in functions or libraries that directly convert integers to binary.

