To enhance the code to handle edge cases like an empty array or a value of `k` greater than the number of unique elements in the array, you can modify the conditionals inside the loop as follows:
```
if (arr.length == 0) {
    return [];
}

if (k > arr.length) {
    k = arr.length;
}

let result = [];
for (let i = 0; i < k; i++) {
    result.push(arr[i]);
}
return result;
```
This modification ensures that the function returns an empty array if the input array is empty, and that the value of `k` is not greater than the number of unique elements in the array. If `k` is greater than the number of unique elements in the array, the function will return all the elements in the array.

Note that this modification assumes that the input array is an array of numbers, and it does not handle other data types like strings or objects.

To optimize the code for the case where 'k' is much smaller than the number of elements in the array, we can use a different approach. Instead of iterating over the entire array and checking each element, we can use a hash table to keep track of the counts of each element. This allows us to quickly look up the count of an element and determine if it is less than or equal to 'k'.
```
function findKthSmallestElement(arr, k) {
  const counts = {};

  // Initialize hash table with element counts
  for (let i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {
    if (!counts[arr[i]]) {
      counts[arr[i]] = 1;
    } else {
      counts[arr[i]]++;
    }
  }

  // Iterate over hash table and find the kth smallest element
  let i = 0;
  for (let key in counts) {
    if (counts[key] <= k) {
      k -= counts[key];
      i++;
    } else {
      return arr[i - 1];
    }
  }

  // If we reach this point, the array is empty or 'k' is greater than the number of elements
  return null;
}
```
This approach has a time complexity of O(n), where n is the length of the input array. It also uses less memory than the previous approach, as it only needs to store a hash table with the counts of each element, rather than an entire copy of the array.

