The code `string result = to_string(3.14);` will not compile because the function `to_string` is only defined for integer types, and `double` is a floating-point type. Therefore, the compiler will not be able to find a suitable overload of the `to_string` function that can handle the `double` argument.

If you want to convert a `double` value to a `string`, you can use the `std::ostringstream` class instead:
```
#include <sstream>

// ...

std::ostringstream oss;
oss << 3.14;
string result = oss.str();
```
This will work because the `std::ostringstream` class has a `<<` operator that can handle any type, including floating-point types like `double`.

Thank you for pointing out these issues with the current implementation of the scheduler. Here are some suggestions to address them:

1. Sorted tasks: Instead of sorting the tasks every time the `run` method is called, we can sort the tasks only once when the scheduler starts and then use a binary search algorithm to find the next task to run. This will reduce the overhead of sorting the tasks for each iteration.
2. Handling new tasks added while running: We can add a new method `addTask` that adds a new task to the list of tasks and also updates the priority queue with the new task. When the scheduler is already running, we can update the priority queue using the `remove` and `put` methods of the `PriorityQueue`. This will ensure that the new task is added to the priority queue in a timely manner without affecting the performance of the scheduler.

Here's an updated version of the code that addresses these issues:
```java
import java.util.*;

public class Scheduler {
    private PriorityQueue<Task> tasks;
    private int currentTime;

    public Scheduler() {
        this.tasks = new PriorityQueue<>();
        this.currentTime = 0;
    }

    public void addTask(Task task) {
        tasks.add(task);
    }

    public void run() {
        while (!tasks.isEmpty()) {
            Task nextTask = tasks.remove();
            if (nextTask.getDeadline() <= currentTime) {
                nextTask.run();
            } else {
                tasks.put(nextTask);
            }
            currentTime++;
        }
    }
}
```

To improve the performance of a producer-consumer problem using condition variables, we can use a heap queue to store the tasks produced by the producer thread. The consumer thread will then dequeue from this queue instead of directly accessing the shared variable. This approach helps to reduce contention on the shared variable and improves overall system throughput.

Here is an example of how to implement this using condition variables in C++:
```
#include <iostream>
#include <mutex>
#include <condition_variable>
#include <thread>
#include <vector>

// Define a struct to store the task information
struct Task {
  int id;
  std::string name;
};

// Define a queue to store the tasks
std::queue<Task> task_queue;

// Define a mutex to protect the shared variable
std::mutex mtx;

// Define a condition variable to signal when the queue is not empty
std::condition_variable cv;

void producer() {
  // Loop until the consumer thread is shut down
  while (true) {
    // Acquire the mutex
    std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock(mtx);

    // Check if the queue is full
    if (task_queue.size() == 10) {
      cv.wait(lock);
    }

    // Produce a new task and add it to the queue
    Task task;
    task.id = 0;
    task.name = "Producer";
    task_queue.push(task);

    std::cout << "Produced task: " << task.id << ", " << task.name << std::endl;

    // Signal the condition variable that a new task has been added to the queue
    cv.notify_all();
  }
}

void consumer() {
  // Loop until the producer thread is shut down
  while (true) {
    // Acquire the mutex
    std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock(mtx);

    // Wait for a new task to be added to the queue
    cv.wait(lock);

    // Dequeue the task from the queue and consume it
    Task task = task_queue.front();
    std::cout << "Consumed task: " << task.id << ", " << task.name << std::endl;

    // Remove the task from the queue
    task_queue.pop();
  }
}

int main() {
  // Create a thread for the producer and consumer
  std::thread producer_thread(producer);
  std::thread consumer_thread(consumer);

  // Wait for the threads to complete
  producer_thread.join();
  consumer_thread.join();

  return 0;
}
```
