The producer thread loops indefinitely, acquiring the mutex, checking if the queue is full, producing a new task and adding it to the queue, and signaling the condition variable that a new task has been added. The consumer thread loops indefinitely, acquiring the mutex, waiting for a new task to be added to the queue, dequeuing the task from the queue, consuming it, and removing it from the queue.

By using a heap queue instead of a shared variable, we reduce contention on the shared variable and improve overall system throughput.

