Here is a program that finds the median of two sorted arrays:
```
function findMedian(arr1, arr2) {
  let len1 = arr1.length;
  let len2 = arr2.length;

  if (len1 === 0 && len2 === 0) {
    return null;
  } else if (len1 === 0 || len2 === 0) {
    return findMedian(arr1, []) || findMedian([], arr2);
  }

  let mid1 = Math.floor(len1 / 2);
  let mid2 = Math.floor(len2 / 2);

  if (mid1 % 2 === 0 && mid2 % 2 === 0) {
    // both medians are integers
    return (arr1[mid1] + arr2[mid2]) / 2;
  } else if (mid1 % 2 === 0 && mid2 % 2 !== 0) {
    // median 1 is an integer, median 2 is not
    return arr1[mid1];
  } else if (mid1 % 2 !== 0 && mid2 % 2 === 0) {
    // median 2 is an integer, median 1 is not
    return arr2[mid2];
  } else {
    // both medians are not integers
    return (arr1[mid1] + arr2[mid2]) / 2;
  }
}
```
This program first checks if both arrays are empty. If they are, it returns `null`. Otherwise, it finds the midpoint of each array and calculates the median based on whether the midpoints are even or odd numbers. If one of the medians is an integer and the other is not, it returns the integer. Otherwise, it returns the average of the two medians.

For example, if we have two arrays `arr1 = [1, 2, 3]` and `arr2 = [4, 5, 6]`, the median would be `(3 + 4) / 2 = 3.5`.

Here's an algorithm to sort the given array in descending order with a time complexity of O(n log n):

1. Divide the array into two halves of approximately equal length.
2. Recursively apply step 1 to each half of the array until the size of the smaller half is 1.
3. Merge the sorted arrays in descending order.

Here's an example implementation of this algorithm:
```
function mergeSort(arr) {
  if (arr.length <= 1) return arr;

  // Divide the array into two halves.
  const mid = Math.floor(arr.length / 2);
  const left = arr.slice(0, mid);
  const right = arr.slice(mid);

  // Recursively apply step 1 to each half of the array.
  const sortedLeft = mergeSort(left);
  const sortedRight = mergeSort(right);

  // Merge the sorted arrays in descending order.
  return mergeDescending(sortedLeft, sortedRight);
}

function mergeDescending(left, right) {
  const result = [];

  while (left.length > 0 && right.length > 0) {
    if (left[0] <= right[0]) {
      result.push(right.shift());
    } else {
      result.push(left.shift());
    }
  }

  // Add any remaining elements from the longer array.
  while (left.length > 0) {
    result.push(left.shift());
  }
  while (right.length > 0) {
    result.push(right.shift());
  }

  return result;
}
```
The time complexity of this algorithm is O(n log n), because the recursive calls to `mergeSort` have a time complexity of O(n log n), and the merge step has a time complexity of O(n).

