This problem can be solved using a combination of binary search and a counter variable. The idea is to use binary search to find the position of the missing number, and then increment the counter until it reaches the desired position.

Here's an example implementation:
```
class Solution {
public:
    int kthMissingNumber(vector<int>& nums, int k) {
        int low = 1, high = nums.size();
        while (low < high) {
            int mid = low + (high - low) / 2;
            if (read(mid) == -1) {
                low = mid + 1;
            } else {
                high = mid;
            }
        }
        int missingNumber = low;
        int counter = 0;
        while (counter < k && missingNumber <= nums.size()) {
            if (read(missingNumber) == -1) {
                counter++;
            }
            missingNumber++;
        }
        return missingNumber;
    }

    int read(int index) {
        // API to read the element at the given index, returns -1 if out of bounds
    }
};
```
In this implementation, we use binary search to find the position of the first missing number. We then increment a counter until it reaches the desired position or the end of the array, whichever comes first. The `read` function is used to check whether an element at a given index exists, and returns -1 if it does not.

The time complexity of this solution is `O(log(n))`, where `n` is the size of the input array. This is because we only need to call the `read` function at most `log(n)` times to find the position of the first missing number, and then we increment a counter until it reaches the desired position or the end of the array, whichever comes first.

Here is an example of how you can implement the `CodingKeyMapping` structure:
```
struct CodingKeyMapping {
    private var mapping: [String: String] = [:]

    mutating func addKey(_ key: String) {
        mapping[key] = key.uppercased()
    }

    mutating func removeKey(_ key: String) {
        mapping[key]?.removeAll()
    }

    func encode(data: [String: Any]) -> [String: Any] {
        var encodedData = data
        for (key, value) in data where mapping.keys.contains(key) {
            let codingKey = mapping[key]!
            if let dictionaryValue = value as? [String: Any] {
                encodedData[codingKey] = encode(data: dictionaryValue)
            } else if let arrayValue = value as? [Any] {
                encodedData[codingKey] = arrayValue.map { $0.encoded }
            } else {
                encodedData[codingKey] = value.encoded
            }
        }
        return encodedData
    }

    func decode(data: [String: Any]) -> [String: Any] {
        var decodedData = data
        for (key, value) in data where mapping.values.contains(key) {
            let codingKey = mapping[key]!
            if let dictionaryValue = value as? [String: Any] {
                decodedData[key] = decode(data: dictionaryValue)
            } else if let arrayValue = value as? [Any] {
                decodedData[key] = arrayValue.map { $0.decoded }
            } else {
                decodedData[key] = value.decoded
            }
        }
        return decodedData
    }
}

extension CodingKeyMapping {
    enum CodingKeys: String, CodingKey {
        case foo
        case bar
        case baz
    }
}
```
In this implementation, the `CodingKeyMapping` structure stores a dictionary of coding keys and their corresponding mapped keys. The `addKey(_:)` method adds a new key to the mapping, while the `removeKey(_:)` method removes a key from the mapping.

The `encode(data:)` method takes a dictionary representing the data to be encoded and returns a new dictionary where the keys are replaced with their corresponding coding keys from the mapping. If a key does not have a corresponding coding key, it is omitted from the result.

The `decode(data:)` method takes a dictionary representing the encoded data and returns a new dictionary where the coding keys are replaced with their corresponding keys from the mapping. If a coding key does not have a corresponding key in the data, it is omitted from the result.

Note that this implementation assumes that the data to be encoded and decoded is a dictionary of string keys and values. It also assumes that all the keys in the mapping are uppercased. You may need to modify this implementation to handle other types of data or to support lowercasing or capitalization of the keys.

