Here is a possible class hierarchy in JavaScript that simulates an ecosystem with base class "Animal" and derived classes for specific species:
```
// Base class: Animal
class Animal {
  constructor(species, age, diet, habitat) {
    this.species = species;
    this.age = age;
    this.diet = diet;
    this.habitat = habitat;
  }

  // Method for age progression
  grow() {
    this.age++;
  }

  // Method for diet change
  changeDiet(newDiet) {
    this.diet = newDiet;
  }
}

// Derived classes: specific species
class Lion extends Animal {
  constructor() {
    super("Lion", 1, "Meat", "Grasslands");
  }
}

class Elephant extends Animal {
  constructor() {
    super("Elephant", 2, "Bamboo", "Forests");
  }
}

class Giraffe extends Animal {
  constructor() {
    super("Giraffe", 3, "Leaves", "Grasslands");
  }
}
```
In this example, the base class "Animal" has attributes like species, age, diet, and habitat. The derived classes for specific species inherit these attributes and add their own unique ones. For instance, the "Lion" class inherits from "Animal" and adds a "Meat" diet and a "Grasslands" habitat.

The methods in the base class "Animal" are also inherited by the derived classes, so all species can grow and change their diets. For example, an instance of the "Lion" class can grow its age using the `grow()` method inherited from "Animal", and it can also change its diet to something else using the `changeDiet()` method.

This is just one possible way to implement a class hierarchy in JavaScript for simulating an ecosystem. There are many other ways to design the classes and their relationships, depending on the specific requirements of the simulation.

