Here is an example of how you could create a doubly linked list in Java:
```
public class DoublyLinkedList<T> {
    private Node<T> head;
    private Node<T> tail;

    public void add(T data) {
        Node<T> newNode = new Node<>(data);
        if (head == null) {
            head = newNode;
            tail = newNode;
        } else {
            tail.next = newNode;
            newNode.prev = tail;
            tail = newNode;
        }
    }

    public void remove(T data) {
        Node<T> current = head;
        while (current != null && !data.equals(current.data)) {
            current = current.next;
        }
        if (current == null) {
            return;
        }
        // Remove the node from the list
        if (current.prev != null) {
            current.prev.next = current.next;
        } else {
            head = current.next;
        }
        if (current.next != null) {
            current.next.prev = current.prev;
        } else {
            tail = current.prev;
        }
    }

    public void print() {
        Node<T> current = head;
        while (current != null) {
            System.out.println(current.data);
            current = current.next;
        }
    }
}
```
The `DoublyLinkedList` class has three methods: `add`, `remove`, and `print`. The `add` method adds a new node to the list with the specified data. The `remove` method removes the first occurrence of a node from the list with the specified data. The `print` method prints the contents of the list in order.

The `Node` class is used to represent each node in the list. It has two fields: `data`, which stores the data associated with the node, and `next` and `prev`, which store references to the next and previous nodes in the list. The `DoublyLinkedList` class also has two fields: `head` and `tail`, which store references to the first and last nodes in the list.

Here is an example of how you could use this class:
```
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        DoublyLinkedList<Integer> list = new DoublyLinkedList<>();
        list.add(1);
        list.add(2);
        list.add(3);
        list.remove(2);
        list.print(); // Output: 1, 3
    }
}
```

To create an Angular component that displays an "about us" page with the specified features, we will need to follow these steps:

1. Set up a new Angular project using the Angular CLI.
2. Create a new component called "AboutUsComponent" and add it to the app's module.
3. Implement lazy loading for the content sections by adding a directive to the HTML template that loads the content only when it is scrolled into view.
4. Implement server-side rendering for the about us page using a NodeJS server with Express framework. This will improve performance and SEO.
5. Implement an authentication system using JWT tokens and add login and registration functionality to the component.
6. Implement internationalization support by adding a pipe that translates the content of the about us page based on user preferences.
7. Implement a caching mechanism to store the fetched content from the server, reducing the number of requests made.
8. Write unit tests for the component using a testing framework such as Jest or Karma.

Here's an example of how we can implement the AboutUsComponent:
```typescript
import { Component, OnInit } from '@angular/core';
import { HttpClient } from '@angular/common/http';
import { Observable } from 'rxjs';
import { map } from 'rxjs/operators';

@Component({
  selector: 'app-about-us',
  templateUrl: './about-us.component.html',
  styleUrls: ['./about-us.component.css']
})
export class AboutUsComponent implements OnInit {
  content$: Observable<any>;

  constructor(private http: HttpClient) {}

  ngOnInit(): void {
    this.content$ = this.http.get('https://example.com/about-us')
      .pipe(map((response) => response.json()));
  }

  getContent(index: number): any {
    return this.content$.pipe(
      map((content) => content[index])
    );
  }
}
```
