```
// This is a basic example of how you might implement a strategy pattern in JavaScript

// Define the interface for the strategy objects
interface Strategy {
  execute(): void;
}

// Implement the strategy objects
class ConcreteStrategyA implements Strategy {
  execute() {
    console.log('Executing A');
  }
}

class ConcreteStrategyB implements Strategy {
  execute() {
    console.log('Executing B');
  }
}

// Define the context object that will use the strategy objects
class Context {
  private strategy: Strategy;

  constructor(strategy: Strategy) {
    this.strategy = strategy;
  }

  execute() {
    this.strategy.execute();
  }
}

// Use the context object with different strategy objects
const contextA = new Context(new ConcreteStrategyA());
contextA.execute(); // Output: Executing A

const contextB = new Context(new ConcreteStrategyB());
contextB.execute(); // Output: Executing B
```

In this example, the `Strategy` interface defines the common behavior that all strategy objects must implement. The `ConcreteStrategyA` and `ConcreteStrategyB` classes are concrete implementations of the strategy interface.

The `Context` class is responsible for holding a reference to the current strategy object and providing an execute method that calls the strategy's execute method.

The `execute()` method in the context class can be used with different strategy objects, depending on the specific implementation required by the system. For example, if we wanted to use the `ConcreteStrategyA` strategy for one part of the system and the `ConcreteStrategyB` strategy for another part, we could create two separate instances of the context class with different strategy objects.

