Object-oriented programming (OOP) is a programming paradigm that focuses on modeling real-world objects, such as people, places, and things, in code. It allows developers to create objects that have attributes and methods that represent the properties and behaviors of the object being modeled. OOP offers several advantages over procedural programming, including:

* Encapsulation: In OOP, each object has its own private data and methods, which are used by the object to perform its intended task. This makes it easier to manage and maintain code as the complexity of the system grows.
* Inheritance: Inheriting from a parent class allows for code reuse and specialization of objects. For example, a child class can inherit all the attributes and methods of a parent class and add new ones that are specific to the child class.
* Polymorphism: Polymorphism is the ability of an object to take on multiple forms. In OOP, this means that objects of different classes can be treated as if they were of the same class, allowing for code reuse and flexibility.

Examples of how OOP can be utilized in practical scenarios include:

* E-commerce system: A customer places an order, which is represented by an object. The order has attributes such as product details, customer information, and payment method. The order also has methods to calculate the total price, add items to the cart, and process payments. The product details are represented by objects of different classes, each with its own set of attributes and methods.
* Banking system: A bank account is an object that has attributes such as balance, interest rate, and payment history. The account also has methods to deposit funds, withdraw funds, and calculate interest.

The key principles of OOP are encapsulation, inheritance, and polymorphism. Encapsulation refers to the idea that each object should have its own private data and methods, which are used by the object to perform its intended task. Inheritance allows for code reuse and specialization of objects. Polymorphism is the ability of an object to take on multiple forms, allowing for code reuse and flexibility.

Polymorphism can be applied to solve complex programming problems by allowing objects of different classes to interact with each other in a flexible way. For example, a payment method class could be used to handle various types of payments, such as credit cards, debit cards, or online payments.

Trade-offs involved in using OOP compared to other programming paradigms include code reusability, maintainability, and flexibility. Code reusability refers to the ability to use code written for one class in another class without having to duplicate it. This is a key advantage of OOP as it allows for code reuse and reduces the need for redundant code. Maintainability refers to the ease with which the code can be modified or updated without causing unintended consequences. OOP makes it easier to maintain code as it follows a set of principles that make it easier to understand and modify. Flexibility refers to the ability to adapt the code to changing requirements or new features. OOP allows for flexibility by allowing objects to take on multiple forms and interact with each other in a flexible way.

Code snippets to illustrate the concepts:
```
// Encapsulation example
class BankAccount {
    private $balance;
    private $interestRate;

    public function __construct($balance, $interestRate) {
        $this->balance = $balance;
        $this->interestRate = $interestRate;
    }

    public function deposit($amount) {
        $this->balance += $amount;
    }

    public function withdraw($amount) {
        if ($this->balance >= $amount) {
            $this->balance -= $amount;
        }
    }

    public function calculateInterest() {
        return $this->balance * $this->interestRate / 100;
    }
}

// Inheritance example
class CreditCard extends BankAccount {
    private $creditLimit;

    public function __construct($balance, $interestRate, $creditLimit) {
        parent::__construct($balance, $interestRate);
        $this->creditLimit = $creditLimit;
    }

    public function charge($amount) {
        if ($this->balance >= $amount) {
            $this->balance -= $amount;
        } else {
            throw new Exception("Insufficient balance");
        }
    }
}

// Polymorphism example
class PaymentMethod {
    public function processPayment(BankAccount $account, $amount) {
        if ($account->getBalance() >= $amount) {
            $account->withdraw($amount);
            return true;
        } else {
            return false;
        }
    }
}

// Usage of polymorphism
$paymentMethod = new PaymentMethod();
$bankAccount = new BankAccount(100, 5);
$creditCard = new CreditCard(200, 5, 150);
$paymentMethod->processPayment($bankAccount, 50); // Returns true
$paymentMethod->processPayment($creditCard, 150); // Throws an exception because the credit card has insufficient balance
```
In conclusion, object-oriented programming offers several advantages over procedural programming, including code reusability, maintainability, and flexibility. It allows developers to create objects that have attributes and methods that represent the properties and behaviors of the object being modeled. The key principles of OOP include encapsulation, inheritance, and polymorphism, which can be applied to solve complex programming problems in a flexible way.

// CheckPaymentStatus function takes in a transaction hash and returns the current status of the transaction
function CheckPaymentStatus(txnHash) {
    // Using the provided transaction hash, retrieve the corresponding block from the blockchain
    let txnBlock = GetBlockByTransactionHash(txnHash);

    // If the transaction is not found in any block, return an error message
    if (txnBlock == null) {
        return "The transaction with hash '" + txnHash + "' was not found.";
    }

    // Retrieve the current block height from the blockchain
    let currentHeight = GetCurrentBlockHeight();

    // Calculate the age of the transaction in blocks
    let txnAgeInBlocks = currentHeight - txnBlock.height;

    // If the transaction is still within the mempool, return "Pending" as the status
    if (txnAgeInBlocks < 0) {
        return "Pending";
    }

    // If the transaction has been in the mempool for more than 10 blocks, it is considered orphaned
    if (txnAgeInBlocks > 10) {
        return "Orphaned";
    }

    // Retrieve the current tip of the blockchain from the blockchain
    let tip = GetTip();

    // Calculate the age of the transaction in seconds
    let txnAgeInSeconds = (currentHeight - txnBlock.height) * blockInterval;

    // If the transaction is still within the time window, return "Processing" as the status
    if (txnAgeInSeconds < blockInterval) {
        return "Processing";
    }

    // If the transaction has been in the mempool for more than 10 blocks and has been in the mempool for longer than the time window, it is considered abandoned
    if (txnAgeInBlocks > 10 && txnAgeInSeconds > blockInterval) {
        return "Abandoned";
    }

    // If the transaction has reached the tip of the blockchain, it is considered confirmed
    if (tip.height == txnBlock.height) {
        return "Confirmed";
    }
}
```

This function uses a combination of `GetBlockByTransactionHash` and `GetCurrentBlockHeight` to retrieve the corresponding block for the provided transaction hash, and calculates the age of the transaction in blocks using subtraction. It then checks if the transaction is still within the mempool, orphaned, abandoned, or confirmed based on the calculated age.

The use of hashing in this function allows for efficient searching and retrieval of transactions from the blockchain, as well as the calculation of the age of a transaction in blocks and seconds. The comments in the code provide additional clarity on how hashing is used in the function.

