You are tasked with implementing a function that extracts the URLs from a given JavaScript code snippet. The URLs are embedded within the `Link` components and are represented by the `to` attribute. Your function should return an array of all the extracted URLs.

Write a function `extractURLsFromCode` that takes a string representing the JavaScript code snippet as input and returns an array of URLs extracted from the `to` attribute of the `Link` components.

Function Signature: `function extractURLsFromCode(code: string): string[]`

For example, given the following JavaScript code snippet:
```javascript
<div>
    {mostUsedScriptures.map(s => {
        return (
            <Link to={s.to}>
                <OutlineScriptureChapter />
            </Link>
        );
    })}
</div>
```
The function `extractURLsFromCode` should return `['s.to']`.

Assume the following:
- The input code snippet will be well-formed and contain valid JavaScript syntax.
- The `Link` components will always have a `to` attribute representing the URL.

response:
Here is a solution for the function `extractURLsFromCode`:
```javascript
function extractURLsFromCode(code) {
    const urls = [];
    const ast = acorn.parse(code);
    ast.body.forEach((node) => {
        if (node.type === 'CallExpression' && node.callee.name === 'Link') {
            const toAttribute = node.arguments[0].properties.find((prop) => prop.key.name === 'to');
            if (toAttribute !== undefined) {
                urls.push(toAttribute.value);
            }
        }
    });
    return urls;
}
```
This function uses Acorn to parse the input code into an Abstract Syntax Tree (AST). It then traverses the AST using a depth-first search, looking for `CallExpression` nodes with the name `Link`. When it finds one, it checks if the `to` attribute is present and adds its value to the output array.

This function assumes that the input code snippet will be well-formed and contain valid JavaScript syntax. It also assumes that the `Link` components will always have a `to` attribute representing the URL. If these assumptions are not met, the function may produce incorrect results or fail to extract URLs altogether.

