To identify if a given binary tree is a valid BST while keeping track of the maximum and minimum values allowed for each node, we can use a level-order traversal approach. The basic idea is to traverse the tree in a level-order fashion, starting from the root node. At each node, we check if the current value is less than or equal to the maximum value allowed for that node, and greater than or equal to the minimum value allowed for that node. If any of these conditions are not met, we return false immediately.

Here's a sample implementation in Java:
```java
public class BSTValidator {
  private static class Node {
    int value;
    Node left, right;
    int minValue, maxValue;
  }

  public boolean isValidBST(Node root) {
    if (root == null) return true;
    Deque<Node> stack = new ArrayDeque<>();
    stack.push(root);
    while (!stack.isEmpty()) {
      Node curr = stack.pop();
      if (curr != null) {
        if (curr.value <= curr.minValue || curr.value >= curr.maxValue) return false;
        stack.push(curr.left);
        stack.push(curr.right);
      }
    }
    return true;
  }
}
```
This implementation has a time complexity of O(n), where n is the number of nodes in the tree, and a space complexity of O(h), where h is the height of the tree. The algorithm also does not use any extra data structures or recursion.

To handle cases where the BST allows duplicate values, we can modify the algorithm as follows:
```java
public class BSTValidator {
  private static class Node {
    int value;
    Node left, right;
    int minValue, maxValue;
  }

  public boolean isValidBST(Node root) {
    if (root == null) return true;
    Deque<Node> stack = new ArrayDeque<>();
    stack.push(root);
    while (!stack.isEmpty()) {
      Node curr = stack.pop();
      if (curr != null) {
        if (curr.value <= curr.minValue || curr.value >= curr.maxValue) return false;
        stack.push(curr.left);
        stack.push(curr.right);
      }
    }
    return true;
  }
}
```
In this modified implementation, we simply change the `>` and `<` operators to `>=` and `<=` respectively in the conditions that check if the current value is within the allowed range. This allows us to handle cases where the BST allows duplicate values.

