To create a database in MySQL, you can use the following steps:

1. Connect to your MySQL server using a client such as MySQL Workbench or the command line tool `mysql`.
2. Create a new database by executing the command `CREATE DATABASE <database_name>;` where `<database_name>` is the name you want to give your database. For example, if you want to create a database called "example", you would execute the command `CREATE DATABASE example;`.
3. Once the database has been created, you can use the command `USE <database_name>;` to switch to that database and start interacting with it. For example, if you want to switch to the "example" database you just created, you would execute the command `USE example;`.
4. You can then create tables within your new database by executing SQL commands such as `CREATE TABLE <table_name> (<column1> <data_type>, <column2> <data_type>);` where `<table_name>` is the name you want to give your table and `<column1>` and `<column2>` are the names of the columns in your table. For example, if you wanted to create a table called "employees" with two columns "id" and "name", you would execute the command `CREATE TABLE employees (id int, name varchar(50));`.
5. You can then insert data into your tables by executing SQL commands such as `INSERT INTO <table_name> VALUES (<value1>, <value2>);` where `<value1>` and `<value2>` are the values you want to insert into your table. For example, if you wanted to insert a new row with "id" of 1 and "name" of "John Doe" into your "employees" table, you would execute the command `INSERT INTO employees VALUES (1, 'John Doe');`.
6. You can then query your data by executing SQL commands such as `SELECT * FROM <table_name>;` which will return all rows from the specified table. For example, if you wanted to retrieve all the rows from your "employees" table, you would execute the command `SELECT * FROM employees;`.
7. Finally, you can drop a database by executing the command `DROP DATABASE <database_name>;` which will delete the entire database including all tables and data within it. For example, if you wanted to delete the "example" database, you would execute the command `DROP DATABASE example;`.

Here is an example of a full MySQL script that creates a new database, a table within that database, inserts some data into the table, and then queries the data:
```
CREATE DATABASE example;
USE example;
CREATE TABLE employees (id int, name varchar(50));
INSERT INTO employees VALUES (1, 'John Doe');
INSERT INTO employees VALUES (2, 'Jane Doe');
SELECT * FROM employees;
DROP DATABASE example;
```

