In this form, we use the `required` attribute to indicate that all fields are mandatory. We also use the `min` and `max` attributes for the age field to specify a valid range of values. For the email address field, we use the `email` input type to ensure that it is in a valid email format.

To add form validation, we can use JavaScript to check if all fields have been filled out correctly before submitting the form. We can also display error messages if any fields are left empty or contain invalid data. Here is an example of how this could be implemented:
```
<script>
  // Get references to the form elements
  var name = document.getElementById("name");
  var age = document.getElementById("age");
  var email = document.getElementById("email");
  var phone = document.getElementById("phone");

  // Add event listener to the form's submit button
  document.getElementById("myForm").addEventListener("submit", function(event) {
    // Check if all fields have been filled out correctly
    if (name.value === "" || age.value === "" || email.value === "" || phone.value === "") {
      alert("Please fill out all fields.");
      event.preventDefault();
    } else if (!isValidEmail(email.value)) {
      alert("Invalid email address.");
      event.preventDefault();
    } else if (!isValidPhoneNumber(phone.value)) {
      alert("Invalid phone number.");
      event.preventDefault();
    }
  });

  // Function to check if an email address is valid
  function isValidEmail(email) {
    var re = /^(([^<>()[\]\\.,;:\s@"]+(\.[^<>()[\]\\.,;:\s@"]+)*)|(".+"))@((\[[0-9]{1,3}\.[0-9]{1,3}\.[0-9]{1,3}\.[0-9]{1,3}])|(([a-zA-Z\-0-9]+\.)+[a-zA-Z]{2,}))$/;
    return re.test(String(email).toLowerCase());
  }

  // Function to check if a phone number is valid
  function isValidPhoneNumber(phone) {
    var re = /^(\+?\d{1,3}|\(\d{3}\))([-\s]?)(\d{3})([-\s]?)(\d{4})$/;
    return re.test(String(phone));
  }
</script>
```
In this code, we first get references to the form elements using their `id` attributes. We then add an event listener to the form's submit button that listens for a click event and checks if all fields have been filled out correctly. If any fields are left empty or contain invalid data, we display an error message and prevent the form from being submitted using the `event.preventDefault()` method.

We also define two functions, `isValidEmail` and `isValidPhoneNumber`, to check if an email address is valid and a phone number is valid, respectively. These functions use regular expressions to test whether the input string matches a specific format. If the input string does not match the expected format, the function returns `false`.

