To create a custom file provider class in an Android application, you can follow these steps:

1. Create a new Java file called `KinRecoveryFileProvider.java` in your app's `java` package.
2. Extend the `FileProvider` class provided by the Android Support Library. You can do this by adding the following line to your file:
```
import androidx.core.content.FileProvider;

public class KinRecoveryFileProvider extends FileProvider {
    // Your custom implementation here
}
```
3. In your `AndroidManifest.xml` file, add a new `<provider>` element with the following attributes:
* `android:authorities`: This should be set to the package name of your app followed by `.fileprovider`, e.g., `com.example.myapp.fileprovider`.
* `android:name`: Set this to the fully qualified class name of your custom file provider, e.g., `com.example.myapp.KinRecoveryFileProvider`.
* `android:exported`: This should be set to `false` to prevent other apps from accessing your files without proper authorization.
```
<provider
    android:authorities="com.example.myapp.fileprovider"
    android:name=".KinRecoveryFileProvider"
    android:exported="false" />
```
4. In your `KinRecoveryFileProvider` class, you can now implement the necessary configuration to securely manage and grant access to files within your application's private storage to other apps. This may include defining security permissions for accessing specific files or directories, implementing custom file permissions checking logic, and so on.
```
import android.content.Context;
import android.net.Uri;
import android.support.v4.content.FileProvider;

public class KinRecoveryFileProvider extends FileProvider {
    private static final String TAG = "KinRecoveryFileProvider";

    // Define the directory where your files are stored
    private static final String FILE_DIRECTORY = "/data/data/com.example.myapp/files";

    // Define the MIME types for your files
    private static final String[] MIME_TYPES = {"application/pdf", "image/jpeg"};

    public KinRecoveryFileProvider(Context context) {
        super(context);
    }

    @Override
    public Uri getUriForFile(Context context, File file) {
        // Check the MIME type of the file and return the appropriate content URI
        if (MIME_TYPES.contains(getMimeType(file))) {
            return FileProvider.getUriForFile(context, FILE_DIRECTORY, file);
        } else {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Unsupported MIME type: " + file.getName());
        }
    }

    @Override
    public boolean onCreate() {
        return true;
    }

    @Override
    public void onLowMemory() {
        // Free up memory by removing all files from the cache
        File directory = new File(FILE_DIRECTORY);
        if (directory.exists()) {
            for (File file : directory.listFiles()) {
                file.delete();
            }
        }
    }

    @Override
    public void onTrimMemory(int level) {
        // Free up memory by removing unused files from the cache
        File directory = new File(FILE_DIRECTORY);
        if (directory.exists()) {
            for (File file : directory.listFiles()) {
                if (!file.isUsed()) {
                    file.delete();
                }
            }
        }
    }

    private String getMimeType(File file) {
        // Return the MIME type of the file based on its extension
        String extension = MimeTypeMap.getFileExtensionFromUrl(file.getName());
        if (extension != null && !"".equals(extension)) {
            return MimeTypeMap.getSingleton().getMimeTypeFromExtension(extension);
        } else {
            return null;
        }
    }
}
```
This custom file provider class is now ready to be used in your application. You can access the files within your app's private storage securely by creating a content:// Uri for a file, using the `getUriForFile()` method of the `KinRecoveryFileProvider` class. The `FileProvider` class will enforce the security permissions you've defined, ensuring that only authorized apps can access your files.

