Here is an example of how you could implement the backend functionality for processing the form data and updating the website settings:
```
<?php
// Connect to the database
$conn = new mysqli('localhost', 'username', 'password', 'database');

// Check if there are any errors connecting to the database
if ($conn->connect_error) {
    die("Connection failed: " . $conn->connect_error);
}

// Get the form data from the $_POST array
$websiteName = $_POST['websiteName'];
$logo = $_POST['logo'];
$description = $_POST['description'];

// Validate the input data
if (empty($websiteName)) {
    $errorMessage = 'Please enter a website name.';
} else if (empty($logo)) {
    $errorMessage = 'Please upload a logo image.';
} else if (empty($description)) {
    $errorMessage = 'Please enter a description.';
}

// Update the settings in the database
if ($errorMessage === '') {
    // Build an SQL UPDATE statement to update the website settings
    $sql = "UPDATE websites SET website_name = '$websiteName', logo = '$logo', description = '$description' WHERE id = 1";

    // Execute the SQL statement and check if it was successful
    if ($conn->query($sql) === TRUE) {
        echo 'Settings updated successfully.';
    } else {
        echo 'Error updating settings: ' . $conn->error;
    }
} else {
    echo $errorMessage;
}

// Close the database connection
$conn->close();
?>
```
This script uses the mysqli extension to connect to the database, retrieve the form data from the $_POST array, validate the input, and update the website settings in the database. It also checks for any errors that may occur during the process and displays an error message if necessary.

