To measure the execution time of a block of code without using any built-in timing functions or libraries, you can use the `Date` class in Swift to get the current time before executing the block of code and then again after it has finished executing. The difference between these two times will give you the execution time of the block of code in milliseconds.

Here's an example implementation of the `measureExecutionTime` function:
```swift
func measureExecutionTime(block: () -> Void) -> Double {
    let startTime = Date()
    block()
    let endTime = Date()
    return endTime.timeIntervalSince1970 * 1000 - startTime.timeIntervalSince1970 * 1000
}
```
This function takes a block of code as an input and returns the execution time of that block in milliseconds. The `Date` class is used to get the current time before and after executing the block of code, and the difference between these two times gives us the execution time.

