```
// This function takes a string as input and returns a boolean indicating whether or not the string is a palindrome.
function isPalindrome(str) {
  // Check if the length of the string is greater than 1, since an empty string cannot be a palindrome.
  if (str.length > 1) {
    // Create two pointers for the beginning and end of the string.
    let start = 0;
    let end = str.length - 1;

    // While the start pointer is less than or equal to the end pointer, compare the characters at the start and end pointers.
    while (start <= end) {
      if (str[start] !== str[end]) {
        return false;
      }

      // Increment the start and decrement the end pointers.
      start++;
      end--;
    }
  }

  // Return true since the string is a palindrome if we reach this point.
  return true;
}
```
In this example, each line of code is commented with an explanation of what it does and why it's important. This makes the code more readable and easier to understand for other developers who might be working on the same project.

To apply a mask to an image while optimizing memory usage and ensuring consistent formatting, you can use in-place operations. In-place operations modify the original data structure instead of creating a new one, which can lead to significant memory savings and improved performance.

Here's an example of how you could apply a mask to an image using in-place operations:
```scss
$image = 'path/to/image.jpg';
$mask = 'path/to/mask.png';

// Load the image and mask
$img = Image::make($image);
$mask = Image::make($mask)->resize(null, $img->height(), function ($constraint) {
    $constraint->aspectRatio();
});

// Apply the mask to the image
$img->mask(function ($constraint) use ($mask) {
    $constraint->maskImage($mask);
});
```
In this example, we first load the image and mask using the `Image` class. We then resize the mask to match the height of the original image using the `aspectRatio()` constraint. Finally, we apply the mask to the image using the `mask()` method.

