Here is the JavaScript implementation of the `sum_ord_lower` function:
```javascript
function sum_ord_lower(s) {
    // define the characters of interest
    let vowels = 'aeiou';
    let consonants = 'abcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz'.replace(/[a-z]/g, function (c) {
        return c.toLowerCase();
    }) - vowels;

    // pick characters at odd indices in reverse order
    let odd_index_chars = s.split('').reverse().filter((c, i) => i % 2 === 1);

    // Compute the sum of ordinal values of the interesting characters
    return odd_index_chars.reduce((a, c) => a + c.charCodeAt(0), 0);
}
```
Note that we use `String.prototype.split()` to split the input string into an array of characters, and then `Array.prototype.reverse()` to reverse the order of the array. We also use `Array.prototype.filter()` to filter out non-consonants, and finally `Array.prototype.reduce()` to compute the sum of the ordinal values of the remaining consonants.

Also note that we use `String.prototype.replace()` to replace all vowels with their lowercase counterparts in the input string before computing the set of consonants. This is necessary because the `set` constructor treats each character as a separate element, and we need to ensure that the set only contains the consonants, not the vowels.

