response:
  To reverse a string without using any built-in string functions or extra data structure, we can use a recursive function that iterates through each character of the string and swaps it with the last character in the string. Here's an example implementation:
```cpp
#include <string>

void reverseStringInPlace(std::string& str) {
    if (str.size() <= 1) return; // Base case: If the string has one or fewer characters, do nothing and return.
    char tmp = str[0]; // Temporarily store the first character of the string.
    str[0] = str[str.size() - 1]; // Replace the first character of the string with the last character.
    str[str.size() - 1] = tmp; // Replace the last character of the string with the temporary storage.
    reverseStringInPlace(str.substr(1, str.size() - 2)); // Recursively call the function on the remaining substring.
}
```
This function uses a recursive approach to iterate through each character of the input `std::string` and swap it with the last character in the string. The base case is when the string has one or fewer characters, in which case the function simply returns without doing any work. The function then recursively calls itself on the remaining substring, swapping the first and last characters and reversing the rest of the string until the entire string is reversed.

To use this function, you can call it with a `std::string` object as an argument, like this:
```cpp
int main() {
    std::string myString = "hello world";
    reverseStringInPlace(myString);
    std::cout << myString << std::endl; // Output: "dlrow olleh"
    return 0;
}
```

