Here is an example of a thread-safe implementation of the `increment` method using locks:
```
class Counter {
    private int value;
    private Object lock = new Object();

    public void increment() {
        synchronized (lock) {
            value++;
        }
    }
}
```
In this example, the `increment` method is implemented as a critical section using the `synchronized` keyword. This means that only one thread can execute the code inside the `synchronized` block at a time, ensuring that the variable `value` is updated correctly even if multiple threads are accessing it simultaneously.

The `lock` object is used to coordinate access to the critical section. When a thread wants to enter the critical section, it must first acquire the lock by calling the `wait()` method on the `lock` object. Once the lock has been acquired, the thread can execute the code inside the `synchronized` block, and then release the lock by calling the `notifyAll()` method on the `lock` object.

By using locks in this way, we ensure that only one thread can access the critical section at a time, which helps to prevent race conditions and ensures that the variable `value` is updated correctly.

