Here is an example of how you could create an Angular component that displays a customized welcome message based on the user's location:
```
import { Component, OnInit } from '@angular/core';
import { Geolocation } from '@angular/common';

@Component({
  selector: 'app-welcome',
  templateUrl: './welcome.component.html',
  styleUrls: ['./welcome.component.css']
})
export class WelcomeComponent implements OnInit {
  location = '';
  countryFlag = '';

  constructor(private geolocation: Geolocation) {}

  ngOnInit(): void {
    this.geolocation.getCurrentPosition().then((position) => {
      const coords = position.coords;
      this.location = `${coords.latitude}, ${coords.longitude}`;
      this.countryFlag = `https://www.countryflags.io/${coords.country}/flat/64.png`;
    });
  }
}
```
The component has a `location` property that stores the user's location, and a `countryFlag` property that stores the URL of the country flag image for their location. The `ngOnInit()` method uses the browser's Geolocation API to get the current position of the user, and then sets the `location` and `countryFlag` properties based on the returned coordinates.

Here is an example of how you could use this component in a template:
```
<app-welcome></app-welcome>
```
The above template will display the welcome message for the user's location, including their country flag image.

It's important to note that this component requires the `@angular/common` module, which provides the Geolocation API, and the `countryflags.io` website, which provides the country flags images.

```
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
  <head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <title>Geolocation Fetch</title>
  </head>
  <body>
    <button id="getLocationButton">Get Location</button>
    <p id="location"></p>

    <script>
      // Get the button element and add an event listener for click events
      const getLocationButton = document.getElementById("getLocationButton");
      getLocationButton.addEventListener("click", function() {
        // Ask for permission to access the user's location
        if (navigator.geolocation) {
          navigator.geolocation.getCurrentPosition(function(position) {
            // If the user grants permission, display their country on the page
            document.getElementById("location").innerHTML = position.coords.country;
          }, function() {
            // If the user denies permission or there is an error, display a message
            document.getElementById("location").innerHTML = "Error: Unable to retrieve your location";
          });
        } else {
          document.getElementById("location").innerHTML = "Error: Geolocation is not supported by this browser";
        }
      });
    </script>
  </body>
</html>
```

This code uses the `navigator.geolocation` API to retrieve the user's location and display their country on the page. When the button is clicked, the `getCurrentPosition()` method is called with two callback functions: one for when the position is retrieved successfully, and another for when there is an error or the user denies permission.

The first callback function, which is passed to `getCurrentPosition()`, displays the country of the user on the page by setting the innerHTML property of the `<p>` element with ID "location".

The second callback function, which is also passed to `getCurrentPosition()`, handles errors or denied permissions. It sets the innerHTML property of the `<p>` element with ID "location" to a message indicating that there was an error retrieving the user's location.

Finally, if the browser does not support geolocation, it sets the innerHTML property of the `<p>` element with ID "location" to a message indicating that geolocation is not supported by this browser.

