// Permutes the given string str of length n using a recursive approach.
void permute(char* str, int n) {
    if (n == 0) {
        printf("%s\n", str);
        return;
    }
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        swap(&str[i], &str[0]);
        permute(str + 1, n - 1);
        swap(&str[i], &str[0]);
    }
}

// Driver function to test the above function.
int main() {
    char* str = "abc";
    int n = strlen(str);
    permute(str, n);
    return 0;
}
```
This program uses a recursive approach to generate permutations of a given string. The `permute()` function takes two arguments: the first is the input string, and the second is its length. The function recursively calls itself with each character removed from the original string until only one character remains. In such a case, the function prints the remaining string.

When the function returns to the recursive call for a particular character, it swaps that character back with the first character of the string and continues with the next character. The `swap()` function is used to perform this task.

The program uses the `strlen()` function to determine the length of the input string, which is then passed as an argument to the `permute()` function. The driver function prints a newline after each permutation is generated.

The issue you're experiencing is due to the fact that Next.js 13 has introduced an experimental feature called `Automatic Static Optimization` which, by default, marks all pages as server-side rendered (SSR) and requires explicit client-side hydration for any page that needs to use state or other client-only functionality.

To resolve this issue, you can disable Automatic Static Optimization in your `next.config.js` file by adding the following configuration:
```javascript
module.exports = {
  experimental: {
    automaticStaticOptimization: false,
  },
};
```
With this configuration, Next.js will no longer automatically optimize your pages as static HTML files and instead render them on the server-side using `getServerSideProps` or `getStaticProps`. This means that you can use any client-only functionality (such as state) in your page without any issues.

However, disabling Automatic Static Optimization also means that Next.js will no longer automatically generate static HTML files for your pages, which may not be desirable for all projects. In such cases, you can use the `getStaticProps` or `getServerSideProps` function to manually fetch and provide data to your page, and then use the `useState` hook in a functional component to store the data locally.

Here's an example of how you could modify your code to use state in your root layout:
```jsx
import { useState } from 'react';
import { getServerSideProps } from 'next';

function RootLayout({ data }) {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);

  return (
    <div>
      <p>You clicked {count} times</p>
      <button onClick={() => setCount(count + 1)}>Click me</button>
      <p>Data from getServerSideProps: {data}</p>
    </div>
  );
}

export const getServerSideProps = async () => {
  const res = await fetch('https://...'); // Replace with your API call
  const data = await res.json();

  return { props: { data } };
};
```
In this example, we're using the `useState` hook to store a count state variable locally and then increment it when the button is clicked. We're also fetching some data from an API endpoint (replace with your own) and passing it as a prop to the component. Finally, we're using the `getServerSideProps` function to manually fetch the data for the page and pass it as a prop to the component.

Note that this approach may not be suitable for all projects, depending on your use case. For example, if you need to fetch data from an API endpoint frequently, you may want to consider using `getStaticProps` instead of `getServerSideProps` to reduce the number of server-side renders required.

In any case, disabling Automatic Static Optimization in your `next.config.js` file should help you achieve what you're looking for.

A breadth-first search (BFS) is an algorithm for traversing or searching tree or graph data structures. It visits all the vertices in a tree level by level, starting from a given vertex. The vertices are visited in the order they were discovered during the search.

The BFS algorithm works as follows:

1. Create a queue to hold the vertices to be searched.
2. Enqueue the starting vertex.
3. Dequeue a vertex and visit it. If the dequeued vertex has not been visited before, mark it as visited and enqueue all its neighbors that have not been visited yet.
4. Repeat step 3 until the queue is empty.

The time complexity of the BFS algorithm is O(V+E), where V is the number of vertices and E is the number of edges in the graph. The space complexity is O(V), as we need to maintain a queue of size V to keep track of the vertices to be searched.

Here is some sample Java code for implementing BFS:
```java
public class BreadthFirstSearch {
  public static void bfs(Graph g, int start) {
    Queue<Integer> queue = new LinkedList<>();
    boolean[] visited = new boolean[g.size()];

    // mark the starting vertex as visited and enqueue it
    visited[start] = true;
    queue.add(start);

    while (!queue.isEmpty()) {
      int v = queue.remove();
      System.out.println("Visiting vertex " + v);
      for (int w : g.neighbors(v)) {
        if (!visited[w]) {
          visited[w] = true;
          queue.add(w);
        }
      }
    }
  }
}
```
This code uses a `Graph` class to represent the graph and a `Queue` to implement the BFS algorithm. The `bfs()` method takes two parameters: the `Graph` object representing the graph, and the starting vertex. It marks all the vertices as unvisited, enqueues the starting vertex, and repeats steps 2-3 until the queue is empty.

Note that this code assumes that the graph is represented as an adjacency list, where each vertex has a list of its neighbors. The `Graph` class could be modified to support other representations if needed.

[PYTHON]
def get_connected_components(graph):
    # Create a copy of the input graph 
    visited = set()
    components = []

    for node in graph:
        if node not in visited:
            component = []
            dfs(graph, node, visited, component)
            components.append(component)

    return components

def dfs(graph, node, visited, component):
    visited.add(node)
    component.append(node)

    for neighbor in graph[node]:
        if neighbor not in visited:
            dfs(graph, neighbor, visited, component)
