Here is a possible implementation of the algorithm you described, with more detailed comments:
```scss
$total-width: 100px;
$num-items: 5;
$item-size: $total-width / $num-items;

// Calculate the total height required to display all items
$total-height: 0;
@for $i from 1 through $num-items {
  $image-height: map-get($images, $i);
  $total-height: $total-height + $image-height;
}

// Create a new array with the total height and number of items
$new-array = [];
$new-array['total_height'] = $total-height;
$new-array['num_items'] = $num-items;

// Calculate the size of each item based on the total width and number of items
@for $i from 1 through $num-items {
  $image-width: map-get($images, $i);
  $new-array['item_size_#{$i}'] = $image-width / $total-width * 100;
}
```
In this implementation, we start by defining the total width of the container and the number of items to be displayed. We then use a for loop to calculate the total height required to display all items in the array. We create a new array with the total height and number of items, as well as the size of each item based on the total width and number of items.

Here is an explanation of each step:

1. `$total-width` is set to 100px. This is the total width of the container in which we want to display the images.
2. `$num-items` is set to 5. This is the number of items we want to display in the array.
3. `$item-size` is calculated as $total-width / $num-items. This is the size of each item based on the total width and number of items.
4. We create a new array called `$new-array` with two properties: `total_height` and `num_items`. The value of `total_height` is calculated as the sum of all the values in the `$images` array, while the value of `num_items` is set to 5.
5. We use a for loop to iterate over the items in the `$images` array. For each item, we calculate its height and add it to the `total_height` property of the `$new-array`. We also calculate the size of each item based on the total width and number of items, and set this value as the `item_size_#{$i}` property in the `$new-array`.
6. Finally, we return the `$new-array`. This array contains all the information needed to display the images in the correct proportions.

Note that this implementation assumes that the values in the `$images` array are all positive integers. If the values can be negative or non-integer, additional checks may be necessary to ensure that the calculations work correctly.

