Here is a Java function that takes a string and searches for the occurrence of a given character, using an iterative approach with a time complexity of O(n):
```
public static int findCharacter(String str, char ch) {
    int index = 0;

    while (index < str.length()) {
        if (str.charAt(index) == ch) {
            return index;
        }
        index++;
    }

    return -1;
}
```
This function iterates through the characters in the given string until it finds a match for the given character. If a match is found, the function returns the index of the matched character. Otherwise, it returns -1 to indicate that no match was found.

If you want to improve the time complexity of this function, you can use a more efficient algorithm such as a binary search or a hash table-based approach. Here is an example of how you could implement a binary search algorithm:
```
public static int findCharacter(String str, char ch) {
    int low = 0;
    int high = str.length() - 1;

    while (low <= high) {
        int mid = (low + high) / 2;
        char c = str.charAt(mid);

        if (c == ch) {
            return mid;
        } else if (c < ch) {
            low = mid + 1;
        } else {
            high = mid - 1;
        }
    }

    return -1;
}
```
This function uses a binary search algorithm to find the index of the first occurrence of the given character in the string. It starts by initializing two indices, `low` and `high`, which define the range of characters to be searched. The function then iteratively searches for the match, using the `mid` value as an anchor point and updating the `low` and `high` values accordingly. If a match is found, the function returns the index of the matched character. Otherwise, it returns -1 to indicate that no match was found.

