To optimize the algorithm, we can eliminate one of the nested loops by using a technique called "duplicate elimination." In this approach, we maintain a separate data structure (e.g., a hash table or an array) to keep track of all the duplicates that have been seen so far. This allows us to remove duplicates more efficiently and reduce the time complexity of the algorithm to O(n log n).

Here is an example of how this optimization can be implemented:
```
function quicksort(arr) {
  // Create a hash table to keep track of all duplicates
  const dups = {};

  // Loop through the array and check for duplicates
  for (let i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {
    const elem = arr[i];

    if (!dups[elem]) {
      dups[elem] = true;
    } else {
      // If the element is a duplicate, remove it from the array
      delete arr[i--];
    }
  }

  // Sort the remaining elements using the quicksort algorithm
  quicksort(arr);
}
```
