```
public class ReverseString {
    public static String reverse(String str) {
        char[] chars = str.toCharArray();
        int left = 0;
        int right = chars.length - 1;
        while (left < right) {
            char temp = chars[left];
            chars[left] = chars[right];
            chars[right] = temp;
            left++;
            right--;
        }
        return new String(chars);
    }
}
```
This Java program uses a simple approach to reverse a string. It first converts the input `String` to an array of `char` using the `toCharArray()` method. Then, it uses two pointers, `left` and `right`, to traverse the array in opposite directions. At each iteration, the character at `chars[left]` is swapped with the character at `chars[right]`, and then both pointers are incremented or decremented by one. This process continues until both pointers meet in the middle of the array. Finally, the reversed string is created by converting the array back to a `String` using the constructor that takes an array of characters.

This program has a time complexity of O(n) because it only iterates through each character once. It also has a space complexity of O(1) because it only requires a constant amount of memory, regardless of the length of the input `String`.

