    $previous-value: $previous-previous-value + $current-value;
    $previous-previous-value: $current-value;
  }

  @return $previous-value;
}
```
This function uses two variables to store the previous and previous previous values of the series, instead of storing all previous values in a list. This makes the function more efficient and reduces the amount of memory it requires.

However, this function is still O(n^2) in time complexity because it calculates each value of the series twice: once to calculate the current value and again to calculate the previous value. To improve the time complexity, we could modify the function to use a single variable to store both the current and previous values of the series:
```scss
@function sum-of-squares($n) {
  $current-value: 0;
  $previous-value: 0;

  @for $i from 1 through $n {
    $current-value: $previous-value + ($i * $i);
    $previous-value: $current-value;
  }

  @return $previous-value;
}
```
This function is now O(n) in time complexity because it only calculates each value of the series once.

