```
public int getSumOfUniquePrimeElements(int[] array) {
    Set<Integer> uniquePrimes = new HashSet<>();
    for (int i : array) {
        if (isPrime(i)) {
            uniquePrimes.add(i);
        }
    }
    int sum = 0;
    for (int prime : uniquePrimes) {
        sum += prime;
    }
    return sum;
}

private boolean isPrime(int n) {
    if (n < 2) {
        return false;
    }
    for (int i = 2; i * i <= n; i++) {
        if (n % i == 0) {
            return false;
        }
    }
    return true;
}
```
The `getSumOfUniquePrimeElements` method takes an array of integers and returns the sum of the unique prime elements in the array. It first creates a set to store all the unique prime elements in the array using the `isPrime` method to check if each element is prime or not. Then it iterates through the set and calculates the sum of all the prime elements.

The `isPrime` method checks if an integer is prime by dividing it by every number between 2 and its square root using a loop. If any divisor is found, the method returns false, indicating that the number is not prime. Otherwise, it returns true, indicating that the number is prime.

You can test this function with the following code:
```
int[] array = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 7, 3, 2};
System.out.println(getSumOfUniquePrimeElements(array)); // Output: 18
```

You can use the following algorithm to find the smallest positive number that is a multiple of 3 and greater than 5, inside the array:

1. Start by initializing a variable `smallestPositiveMultipleOfThreeGreaterThanFive` to -1. This will be the return value if no such number exists in the array.
2. Iterate through each element of the array using a for loop.
3. For each element, check if it is greater than 5 and divisible by 7 and a prime number. If so, set `smallestPositiveMultipleOfThreeGreaterThanFive` to that element.
4. After iterating through all elements of the array, return `smallestPositiveMultipleOfThreeGreaterThanFive`.

Here is an example implementation in Java:
```
public int findSmallestPositiveMultipleOfThreeGreaterThanFive(int[] array) {
    int smallestPositiveMultipleOfThreeGreaterThanFive = -1;

    for (int i = 0; i < array.length; i++) {
        if (array[i] > 5 && array[i] % 7 == 0 && isPrime(array[i])) {
            smallestPositiveMultipleOfThreeGreaterThanFive = array[i];
            break;
        }
    }

    return smallestPositiveMultipleOfThreeGreaterThanFive;
}

private boolean isPrime(int number) {
    if (number < 2) {
        return false;
    }

    for (int i = 2; i * i <= number; i++) {
        if (number % i == 0) {
            return false;
        }
    }

    return true;
}
```
This algorithm first initializes a variable `smallestPositiveMultipleOfThreeGreaterThanFive` to -1, which will be the return value if no such number exists in the array. Then it iterates through each element of the array using a for loop. For each element, it checks if it is greater than 5 and divisible by 7 and a prime number using the `isPrime` method. If so, it sets `smallestPositiveMultipleOfThreeGreaterThanFive` to that element. Finally, after iterating through all elements of the array, it returns `smallestPositiveMultipleOfThreeGreaterThanFive`.

The `isPrime` method checks if a given number is prime by iterating from 2 up to the square root of the number and checking if the number is divisible by any of those numbers. If no such number exists, then the number must be prime.

For example, if the input array is [-9, -6, -3, 1, 2, 4, 7, 8, 9], the function would return 9, since it is the smallest positive multiple of 3 and greater than 5 that is divisible by 7 and a prime number. If there is no such number in the array, then the function would return -1.

Here is an example of how you could design a text-based game in C++ where the player can move around the game world:
```
#include <iostream>
#include <string>

using namespace std;

// Define the different directions the player can move
enum Direction {
    NORTH,
    SOUTH,
    EAST,
    WEST
};

int main() {
    // Initialize the game world
    int width = 50;
    int height = 50;
    char gameWorld[width][height];
    for (int i = 0; i < width; i++) {
        for (int j = 0; j < height; j++) {
            gameWorld[i][j] = ' ';
        }
    }

    // Define the player's starting position
    int playerX = 25;
    int playerY = 25;

    // Main loop of the game
    while (true) {
        // Display the current game world to the player
        cout << "You are at (" << playerX << ", " << playerY << ")";
        for (int i = 0; i < width; i++) {
            for (int j = 0; j < height; j++) {
                if (gameWorld[i][j] == ' ') {
                    cout << "  ";
                } else {
                    cout << gameWorld[i][j];
                }
            }
            cout << endl;
        }
        cout << endl;

        // Get the player's input
        string input;
        cin >> input;

        // Process the player's input
        if (input == "n") {
            movePlayer(NORTH);
        } else if (input == "s") {
            movePlayer(SOUTH);
        } else if (input == "e") {
            movePlayer(EAST);
        } else if (input == "w") {
            movePlayer(WEST);
        } else {
            cout << "Invalid input. Please try again." << endl;
        }
    }

    return 0;
}

// Function to move the player in a specific direction
void movePlayer(Direction dir) {
    switch (dir) {
        case NORTH:
            if (playerY - 1 >= 0) {
                playerY--;
            } else {
                cout << "You can't go further." << endl;
            }
            break;
        case SOUTH:
            if (playerY + 1 < height) {
                playerY++;
            } else {
                cout << "You can't go further." << endl;
            }
            break;
        case EAST:
            if (playerX + 1 < width) {
                playerX++;
            } else {
                cout << "You can't go further." << endl;
            }
            break;
        case WEST:
            if (playerX - 1 >= 0) {
                playerX--;
            } else {
                cout << "You can't go further." << endl;
            }
            break;
    }
}
```
This code creates a text-based game where the player can move around a 50x50 grid. The player starts at position (25, 25) and can move in four directions: north, south, east, and west. If the player tries to move further than the boundaries of the grid, they are notified that they cannot go further.

You will need to add additional code to handle collisions with objects in the game world, as well as to update the game state based on player actions.

