To implement the `push` method for the `JsHeap` struct, you will need to use the provided `HeapFragment` struct to allocate memory on the JavaScript heap. The `HeapFragment` struct provides methods for allocating and deallocating memory from the JavaScript heap.

Here's an example implementation of the `push` method that should fulfill the memory management requirements:
```rust
#[wasm_bindgen(constructor)]
pub fn new(size: usize) -> JsHeap {
    let fragment = HeapFragment::new_from_word_size(size).unwrap();
    JsHeap {
        fragment,
        terms: Vec::new(),
    }
}

fn push(&mut self, term: Term) -> usize {
    // Check if the heap has enough space to allocate the new term
    let mut current_size = 0;
    for (index, _) in self.terms.iter().enumerate() {
        current_size += index.term_size();
    }

    if current_size + term.term_size() > self.fragment.word_size() {
        // Return an error if the heap does not have enough space to allocate the new term
        return Err(JsValue::from_str("Heap exhausted"));
    }

    // Allocate memory for the new term on the JavaScript heap
    let address = self.fragment.allocate(term.term_size());

    if address == -1 {
        // Return an error if the allocation failed
        return Err(JsValue::from_str("Allocation failed"));
    }

    // Add the new term to the heap and return its index
    self.terms.push(term);
    let index = self.terms.len() - 1;
    self.fragment.set_value(address, term.to_string());
    Ok(index)
}
```
