Yes, it is possible to use breadth-first search (BFS) to find the shortest path in a graph. BFS is a traversal algorithm that explores all the nodes of a graph level by level, starting from a given source node. The algorithm marks each node it visits with a color, and when it finishes exploring a particular level, it moves on to the next one.

To find the shortest path in a graph using BFS, you can use the following steps:

1. Start by selecting a source node in the graph. This node will be used as the starting point for the search.
2. Initialize a queue data structure to hold the nodes that need to be explored. Enqueue the source node.
3. While the queue is not empty, dequeue a node from it and mark it as visited. For each of its neighbors that have not been visited, compute the distance between them and the current node. If the computed distance is less than or equal to the previously recorded shortest path distance for that neighbor, update the shortest path distance and the previous node for that neighbor.
4. If a neighbor has been visited but its shortest path distance has not been updated, it means that there is no path from the source node to that neighbor. In this case, you can dequeue the neighbor and mark it as unvisited.
5. Once all the nodes in the queue have been explored, return the shortest path from the source node to the target node, which is stored in the previous node of each visited node.

The optimized code using BFS would look like this:
```java
import java.util.*;

public class ShortestPath {
    public static int[] findShortestPath(Graph graph, int startNode, int endNode) {
        // Initialize a queue to hold the nodes that need to be explored
        Queue<Integer> queue = new LinkedList<>();
        // Mark all the nodes as unvisited
        boolean[] visited = new boolean[graph.getNumberOfNodes()];
        for (int i = 0; i < graph.getNumberOfNodes(); i++) {
            visited[i] = false;
        }
        // Initialize a variable to hold the shortest path distance
        int minDistance = Integer.MAX_VALUE;
        int previousNode = -1;
        // Start with the source node
        queue.add(startNode);
        while (!queue.isEmpty()) {
            // Dequeue the first node from the queue
            int currentNode = queue.poll();
            // If the node has not been visited, mark it as visited and enqueue its neighbors
            if (!visited[currentNode]) {
                visited[currentNode] = true;
                for (int neighbor : graph.getNeighbors(currentNode)) {
                    if (!visited[neighbor]) {
                        queue.add(neighbor);
                    }
                }
            // If the node has been visited and its distance is less than or equal to the previously recorded shortest path distance, update it
            } else if (graph.getDistance(currentNode) <= minDistance) {
                previousNode = currentNode;
                minDistance = graph.getDistance(currentNode);
            }
        }
        // Return the shortest path from the source node to the target node
        return findPath(previousNode, endNode, new int[graph.getNumberOfNodes()]);
    }

    private static int[] findPath(int currentNode, int endNode, int[] path) {
        // If we have reached the target node, return the path
        if (currentNode == endNode) {
            return path;
        }
        // Otherwise, explore each of its neighbors and find a path to them
        for (int neighbor : graph.getNeighbors(currentNode)) {
            if (!visited[neighbor]) {
                visited[neighbor] = true;
                int[] newPath = findPath(neighbor, endNode, path);
                // If we found a path to one of the neighbors, return it
                if (newPath != null) {
                    return newPath;
                }
            }
        }
        // If none of the neighbors have a path, return null
        return null;
    }
}
```

