// Q# code for quantum teleportation
// This code is based on the quantum teleportation algorithm proposed by Einstein, Brout, and Huang in 1993.

operation Teleport(qubit_in : Qubit, qubit_out : Qubit) : Result {
    // Measurement of the input qubit to determine its state
    let r = M(qubit_in);

    // If the input qubit is in a superposition state, apply a controlled operation
    // to the output qubit to entangle it with the input qubit.
    if (r == One) {
        Apply((Controlled X), [qubit_out], [qubit_in]);
    }

    // Return the measurement result as an int
    return r;
}
```
This code sample demonstrates a simple quantum teleportation operation in Q#, which is based on the classical algorithm proposed by Einstein, Brout, and Huang in 1993. The operation takes two qubits as inputs: one representing the data to be teleported (qubit_in) and another representing the reference qubit for entanglement (qubit_out).

The operation first measures the state of the input qubit using the classical `M` function. If the measured result is a superposition state, it applies a controlled operation to entangle the output qubit with the input qubit. This entangled state represents the teleported quantum state on the second qubit.

The operation then returns the measurement result as an int value. The return type of the operation is `Result`, which indicates whether the measured state was a superposition state (1) or a definite state (0).

This code sample illustrates how to implement a simple quantum teleportation algorithm using Q# and demonstrates the fundamental principles behind this process.

Here's an example implementation of a Java program that simulates a deck of cards using an enum:
```
import java.util.*;

public class CardGame {
    private static final int NUM_SUITS = 4;
    private static final int NUM_RANKS = 13;
    private static final int MAX_CARDS = NUM_SUITS * NUM_RANKS;

    enum Suit {
        HEARTS, DIAMONDS, CLUBS, SPADES;

        public String toString() {
            return name().toLowerCase();
        }
    }

    enum Rank {
        ACE(1), TWO(2), THREE(3), FOUR(4), FIVE(5), SIX(6), SEVEN(7), EIGHT(8), NINE(9), TEN(10), JACK(10), QUEEN(10), KING(10);

        private final int value;

        Rank(int value) {
            this.value = value;
        }

        public int getValue() {
            return value;
        }
    }

    static class Card {
        Suit suit;
        Rank rank;

        public Card(Suit suit, Rank rank) {
            this.suit = suit;
            this.rank = rank;
        }

        @Override
        public String toString() {
            return "[" + rank.toString() + " of " + suit.toString() + "]";
        }
    }

    static class Deck {
        private List<Card> cards;
        private Random random = new Random();

        public Deck() {
            cards = new ArrayList<>();
            for (Suit suit : Suit.values()) {
                for (Rank rank : Rank.values()) {
                    cards.add(new Card(suit, rank));
                }
            }
        }

        public void shuffle() {
            Collections.shuffle(cards, random);
        }

        public Card dealCard() {
            if (cards.isEmpty()) {
                return null;
            } else {
                return cards.remove(0);
            }
        }

        public void printCards() {
            for (Card card : cards) {
                System.out.println(card);
            }
        }

        public int getValue(Card card) {
            return card.rank.getValue();
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Deck deck = new Deck();

        while (true) {
            System.out.println("Shuffling the deck...");
            deck.shuffle();

            System.out.println("Dealing a card from the top of the deck:");
            Card card = deck.dealCard();
            if (card == null) {
                System.out.println("No cards left in the deck!");
                break;
            } else {
                System.out.println(card);
            }

            System.out.println("Printing remaining cards in the deck:");
            deck.printCards();

            System.out.println("Determining value of a given card:");
            Card testCard = new Card(Suit.CLUBS, Rank.ACE);
            int value = deck.getValue(testCard);
            System.out.println("The value of " + testCard + " is " + value + ".");
        }
    }
}
```
This program simulates a deck of cards with 4 suits (hearts, diamonds, clubs, and spades) and 13 ranks (Ace through King). It uses an enum to represent the suit and rank of each card. The `Card` class is used to represent a specific card in the deck.

