function extract_links(text) {
  // Sanitize the input to prevent potential security issues
  text = text.replace(/&amp;/g, "&");
  text = text.replace(/&lt;/g, "<");
  text = text.replace(/&gt;/g, ">");

  // Extract links from the text using a regular expression
  const regex = /https?:\/\/(www\.)?[-a-zA-Z0-9@:%._\+~#=]{2,256}\.[a-z]{2,6}\b([-a-zA-Z0-9@:%_\+.~#?&//=]*)/g;
  const matches = text.match(regex);

  // Return an array of links found in the text
  return matches || [];
}
```
In this version, we first sanitize the input by replacing any HTML entities (like `&amp;` or `&lt;`) with their corresponding characters using `replace()`. This helps prevent potential security issues if the input contains malicious code.

Next, we extract links from the text using a regular expression. The regular expression `/https?:\/\/(www\.)?[-a-zA-Z0-9@:%._\+~#=]{2,256}\.[a-z]{2,6}\b([-a-zA-Z0-9@:%_\+.~#?&//=]*)/g` matches any URL that starts with `http://`, `https://`, or `www.`. The `\b` at the end of the regular expression ensures that we only match URLs that are followed by a space or the end of the text.

Finally, we return an array of links found in the text using the `match()` method. If no matches were found, the function returns an empty array.

By sanitizing the input and using a more robust regular expression, this version of the function should be more reliable and easier to maintain.

