public class MinStack {
    private Stack<Integer> stack = new Stack<>();
    private Stack<Integer> minStack = new Stack<>();

    public void push(int x) {
        stack.push(x);
        if (minStack.isEmpty() || x < minStack.peek()) {
            minStack.push(x);
        } else {
            minStack.push(minStack.peek());
        }
    }

    public void pop() {
        stack.pop();
        minStack.pop();
    }

    public int getMinimum() {
        return minStack.peek();
    }
}
```
In this implementation, the `minStack` keeps track of the minimum element in the `stack`. When you push an element onto the stack, you check if it's less than the current minimum element in the `minStack`. If it is, then you push the new element onto the `minStack`. Otherwise, you push the current minimum element onto the `minStack`.

When you pop an element from the stack, you also need to pop the corresponding element from the `minStack`. This ensures that the `minStack` remains consistent with the state of the `stack`.

The `getMinimum()` method simply returns the top element of the `minStack`, which is the minimum element in the `stack`.

Overall, this approach allows you to achieve O(1) time complexity for the getMinimum method while still maintaining a simple and efficient implementation.

