One way to do this is by using a divide-and-conquer approach, where we recursively sort the subarrays and then merge them together. This can be done by dividing the array into smaller subarrays until each subarray contains only one element, at which point we can simply return the sorted array. The merge step can then be performed in a single pass, which would result in a time complexity of O(n log n).

Here is an example implementation of the `merge_sort` function using this approach:
```
def merge_sort(arr):
    if len(arr) <= 1:
        return arr

    mid = len(arr) // 2
    left = arr[:mid]
    right = arr[mid:]

    left = merge_sort(left)
    right = merge_sort(right)

    return merge(left, right)

def merge(left, right):
    result = []

    while len(left) > 0 and len(right) > 0:
        if left[0] <= right[0]:
            result.append(left.pop(0))
        else:
            result.append(right.pop(0))

    if len(left) > 0:
        result += left
    elif len(right) > 0:
        result += right

    return result
```
This implementation uses a recursive approach to sort the array, with each recursive call dividing the input array into smaller subarrays until each subarray contains only one element. The merge step is then performed in a single pass, which would result in a time complexity of O(n log n).

