However, it looks like [ConfigurationManager](http://msdn.microsoft.com/en-us/library/system.configuration.configurationmanager.aspx) will only give you Configuration instances that are associated with an EXE file or a machine config.  Is there a way to load arbitrary XML into a Configuration instance?

response:
You're correct that the `ConfigurationManager` class is used to retrieve the configuration for the currently executing application, and it can only be used with executables or machine configurations. However, you can still use the `System.Configuration` namespace to create a new `Configuration` object using an arbitrary XML string as the configuration source.

Here's an example of how you can do this:
```csharp
using System;
using System.IO;
using System.Xml;
using System.Configuration;

class Program
{
    static void Main(string[] args)
    {
        string xmlString = "<?xml version=\"1.0\"?><configuration>...</configuration>";
        using (var reader = new StringReader(xmlString))
        {
            var xmlDoc = new XmlDocument();
            xmlDoc.Load(reader);
            Configuration config = Configuration.CreateFromXml(xmlDoc, "MyCustomConfigSection");

            // Use the configuration as needed...
        }
    }
}
```
In this example, we create an `XmlDocument` object from the arbitrary XML string and then use the `Configuration.CreateFromXml` method to create a new `Configuration` instance based on that document. We also pass in a custom section name ("MyCustomConfigSection") as a parameter to the `CreateFromXml` method, which is used to define the root element of the configuration file.

Note that you'll need to use the `using` statement when creating the `StringReader` object, as it implements `IDisposable`. This ensures that the reader is properly disposed of when it's no longer needed.

