Here is an example algorithm that meets the requirements:
```
function mostVowels(sentence) {
  // Initialize variables to keep track of the current word with the most vowels and its count
  let mostVowelsWord = "";
  let mostVowelsCount = 0;

  // Iterate over each word in the sentence
  for (let i = 0; i < sentence.length; i++) {
    // Check if the current word has more vowels than the previous word
    if (countVowels(sentence[i]) > mostVowelsCount) {
      mostVowelsWord = sentence[i];
      mostVowelsCount = countVowels(sentence[i]);
    }
  }

  // Return the word with the most vowels
  return mostVowelsWord;
}

// Count the number of vowels in a given word
function countVowels(word) {
  let count = 0;

  // Iterate over each character in the word
  for (let i = 0; i < word.length; i++) {
    // Check if the current character is a vowel
    if (isVowel(word[i])) {
      count++;
    }
  }

  return count;
}

// Check if a given character is a vowel
function isVowel(char) {
  return char === "a" || char === "e" || char === "i" || char === "o" || char === "u";
}
```
This algorithm uses a for loop to iterate over each word in the sentence, and then checks if the current word has more vowels than the previous word using the `countVowels` function. The `countVowels` function iterates over each character in the word and counts the number of vowels it contains using the `isVowel` function.

The time complexity of this algorithm is O(n), where n is the length of the sentence, because the algorithm only needs to iterate over each word once to find the word with the most vowels. The space complexity is O(1), because the algorithm does not use any additional data structures to store information about the words in the sentence.

