Here's an example of how you could implement this in JavaScript:
```
// Get the user input as a string
let userInput = prompt("Enter a string:");

// Check if the input is a palindrome
function isPalindrome(str) {
  return str === str.split('').reverse().join('');
}

// Validate the user input to ensure it is a string
if (typeof userInput !== "string") {
  alert("Invalid input. Please enter a valid string.");
} else {
  // Remove spaces, punctuation and case sensitivity from the input
  let cleanedUserInput = userInput.toLowerCase().replace(/\s+/g, '').replace(/[^a-zA-Z0-9]+/g, '');

  if (isPalindrome(cleanedUserInput)) {
    alert("Your input is a palindrome.");
  } else {
    alert("Your input is not a palindrome.");
  }
}
```
This program uses the `prompt()` function to get the user input as a string. It then defines a function called `isPalindrome` that takes a string as an argument and returns true if it is a palindrome, false otherwise. The function splits the input into an array of characters, reverses it, and joins it back together to create a new string. Then it checks if the original string is equal to the reversed string.

The program also validates the user input to ensure it is a string by using the `typeof` operator to check if it is a string or not. If the input is not a string, it will alert the user that their input is invalid and ask them to enter another string. If the input is valid, it will remove spaces, punctuation, and case sensitivity from the input by using regular expressions and then call the `isPalindrome` function to check if it is a palindrome or not.

Note that this program uses the `toLowerCase()` method to convert the string to lowercase, which will make the comparison case insensitive. It also uses the `replace()` method to remove any spaces and punctuation from the input before comparing it to the reversed string.

Here is an example of how you can use Java to generate a two-dimensional array containing 1000 strings, each string containing 10 random alphanumeric characters (both lowercase and uppercase), with no repeated strings within the array:
```
import java.util.Random;

public class GenerateUniqueStrings {
  public static void main(String[] args) {
    int numStrings = 1000;
    int stringLength = 10;
    Random random = new Random();

    String[][] strings = new String[numStrings][stringLength];

    for (int i = 0; i < numStrings; i++) {
      // generate a random alphanumeric string of length 10
      String str = "";
      for (int j = 0; j < stringLength; j++) {
        char c = (char) ('a' + random.nextInt(26));
        if (random.nextBoolean()) {
          c += 'A' - 'a';
        }
        str += c;
      }

      // check if the generated string is already in the array
      boolean unique = true;
      for (int j = 0; j < i; j++) {
        if (strings[j][0].equals(str)) {
          unique = false;
          break;
        }
      }

      // add the generated string to the array if it is unique
      if (unique) {
        strings[i][0] = str;
