```
import java.util.List;

public class Pipeline {
  private List<Step> steps;

  public Pipeline(List<Step> steps) {
    this.steps = steps;
  }

  public void execute() {
    for (Step step : steps) {
      step.execute();
    }
  }
}

public interface Step {
  void execute();
}

public class MyStep implements Step {
  @Override
  public void execute() {
    // Perform some action here
  }
}
```
In this example, the `Pipeline` class takes a list of steps as its constructor argument. The `execute()` method then loops through each step and calls its `execute()` method in turn.

The `Step` interface defines an `execute()` method that must be implemented by any classes that want to be used as steps in the pipeline. The `MyStep` class is a concrete implementation of this interface, and it performs some action when its `execute()` method is called.

To use this pattern, you would first define your steps as separate classes that implement the `Step` interface. Then, you can create a pipeline by passing in a list of these step objects to the `Pipeline` constructor. Finally, you can call the `execute()` method on the pipeline object to execute all of its steps in sequence.
