Thank you for the suggestion! Using `requestAnimationFrame` can indeed help optimize the animation for better performance. By using this method, we can ensure that the browser is only rendering the animation when it's ready to display a new frame, rather than constantly repainting the screen with every frame of the animation. This can help reduce the amount of overhead and improve overall performance.

Here's an example of how you could use `requestAnimationFrame` in your code:
```
function animate() {
  // Get the current time
  var now = Date.now();

  // Calculate the elapsed time since the last frame
  var elapsed = now - lastTime;

  // Update the animation based on the elapsed time
  update(elapsed);

  // Draw the next frame of the animation
  draw();

  // Set the last time to the current time
  lastTime = now;
}

function update(elapsed) {
  // Update the position and rotation of the object based on the elapsed time
}

function draw() {
  // Draw the object at its updated position and rotation
}

// Start the animation loop
animate();
```
In this example, we define an `animate` function that will be called repeatedly to update and draw the animation. We also define a `lastTime` variable to keep track of the time of the last frame, which we use to calculate the elapsed time between frames.

Within the `animate` function, we first get the current time using `Date.now()`. We then calculate the elapsed time since the last frame by subtracting the last time from the current time. We pass this elapsed time to an `update` function, which will update the position and rotation of the object based on how much time has passed.

Finally, we call a `draw` function that will draw the object at its updated position and rotation. After updating and drawing the animation, we set the last time to the current time and repeat the process by calling `animate` again. This will create a smooth and efficient animation loop that only updates the animation when it's ready to display a new frame.

By using `requestAnimationFrame`, we can ensure that our animation is running smoothly and efficiently, even on slower devices or with high-resolution displays.

