## [Solution](https://github.com/HackYourFuture/Fundamentals/tree/master/fundamentals/fundamentals_week5/solutions)

# Week 6: Fundamentals of JavaScript

In week six we'll continue our dive into the world of JavaScript. We'll learn about functions, loops and control structures, as well as how to work with arrays, objects and classes.

## Learning Objectives

- Functions: create a function that takes in parameters
- Control Structures: use if/else statements, while loops, for loops and switch statements
- Arrays: create an array, loop over it and access its contents
- Objects: create an object, access its properties and methods
- Classes: create a class, instantiate an object from a class and use its methods

## Prerequisites

- [Node.js](https://nodejs.org/en/) (see [node version](#node-version))
- `npm` (comes with node)
- [Git](https://git-scm.com/)
- [Visual Studio Code](https://code.visualstudio.com/) (or equivalent IDE)
- Basic understanding of HTML, CSS and JavaScript

## Node Version

We recommend using the latest LTS version of [Node.js](https://nodejs.org/en/). You can check your node version by running `node -v` in your terminal or cmd prompt.

> _Note:_ To use npm you need to have a C++ compiler installed on your system. Please refer to the [official npm installation instructions](https://www.npmjs.com/get-npm) for details.

## Homework

- For this homework we'll work with [JavaScript Arrays](https://github.com/HackYourFuture/fundamentals/tree/master/fundamentals/fundamentals_week6/homework).
- By the end of Week 6 you should have completed this homework as well as being able to explain your code to someone else.
- You can find a summary of all the topics we'll cover in [this document](https://github.com/HackYourFuture/fundamentals/tree/master/fundamentals/fundamentals_week6).

## Getting Started

In week six you will get started by completing an array homework assignment. Please fork and clone the repository so that you have a local copy to work on.

## Helpful Resources

- [Mozilla Developer Network](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/) (MDN) is a great resource for learning new programming concepts. They have a comprehensive collection of tutorials, examples and reference materials for web development.

## Submission Instructions

For every homework assignment, please provide a Pull Request on the repository you were given during the class. A maintainer will review your code and may request changes before merging it with the main repository.

Please make sure to run the `npm test` command to ensure that everything is set up correctly. You should see an output like this:

```bash
$ npm test
  ✓ should be able to create a new instance of a Pizza (3ms)
  ✓ should be able to add a topping to pizza (1ms)
  ✓ should be able to return all toppings of a pizza (1ms)
  ✓ should be able to calculate the total price of a pizza (2ms)
```

If your tests are not passing, please try to understand why by using the debugger built into most editors/IDEs. Alternatively you can try running `npm test` with the `--verbose` flag to get more information about what is happening:

```bash
$ npm test -- --verbose
```

## Contributing

Please see [CONTRIBUTING.md](CONTRIBUTING.md) for details on our code of conduct, and the process for submitting pull requests to us.

## Versioning

We use [SemVer](http://semver.org/) for versioning. For the versions available, see the tags on this repository.

## Authors

- **Hack Your Future** - _Lecture and homework by:_ **_Maurice Herman_**

