```java
public static void quickSort(int[] arr) {
    int left = 0;
    int right = arr.length - 1;

    while (left < right) {
        // partition the array around a pivot element
        int pivot = partition(arr, left, right);

        // recursively sort the two sub-arrays on either side of the pivot
        quickSort(arr, left, pivot - 1);
        quickSort(arr, pivot + 1, right);
    }
}

private static int partition(int[] arr, int low, int high) {
    // select a random element as the pivot
    Random rand = new Random();
    int pivotIndex = rand.nextInt(high - low + 1) + low;
    int pivot = arr[pivotIndex];

    // move all elements less than the pivot to the left of it
    int i = low;
    for (int j = low; j < high; j++) {
        if (arr[j] <= pivot) {
            swap(arr, i++, j);
        }
    }

    // move the pivot element to its final position
    swap(arr, i, high);

    return i;
}

private static void swap(int[] arr, int i, int j) {
    int tmp = arr[i];
    arr[i] = arr[j];
    arr[j] = tmp;
}
```
This code uses a random pivot element for each partition, which reduces the risk of getting stuck in an infinite loop. The `partition` method is implemented using a variant of the Hoare partition scheme that moves all elements less than the pivot to the left of it and moves the pivot element to its final position. The `quickSort` method uses this `partition` method to recursively sort the two sub-arrays on either side of the pivot element.

This code is optimized for time complexity by using a random pivot element, which reduces the risk of getting stuck in an infinite loop and also improves the performance of the algorithm. Additionally, the use of a single while loop instead of recursion allows the method to handle large arrays efficiently.

