Here is a sample Java class that performs a binary search on a sorted array of integers without using any built-in search functions or libraries:
```java
import java.util.*;

public class BinarySearch {
  public static int search(int[] arr, int target) {
    int low = 0;
    int high = arr.length - 1;
    while (low <= high) {
      int mid = (low + high) / 2;
      if (arr[mid] == target) {
        return mid;
      } else if (arr[mid] < target) {
        low = mid + 1;
      } else {
        high = mid - 1;
      }
    }
    return -1; // target not found
  }
}
```
This class contains a single method `search` that takes an array of integers `arr` and a target element `target` as input, and returns the index of the first occurrence of `target` in `arr`. If `target` is not found in `arr`, the method returns `-1`. The method uses a binary search algorithm to find the target element.

The time complexity of this method is O(log n), where n is the number of elements in the array. This is because the method recursively divides the search space in half with each iteration, until it finds the target element or determines that it is not present in the array. The binary search algorithm has a worst-case time complexity of O(log n) for sorted arrays.

This class can handle arrays with up to 10^9 elements because it uses a signed 32-bit integer to represent the indices of the array. This allows it to handle arrays that are up to 2^31 - 1 elements in length, which is more than enough for most practical purposes. However, if you need to handle arrays with even larger sizes, you may need to use a different data type or an external library to represent the indices of the array.

