Here's a solution in O(MxN) time and without using any extra space:
```
public static void printSpiral(int[][] matrix) {
    int m = matrix.length;
    int n = matrix[0].length;
    int top = 0, bottom = m - 1, left = 0, right = n - 1;
    while (top <= bottom && left <= right) {
        // print the top row
        for (int i = left; i <= right; i++) {
            System.out.print(matrix[top][i] + " ");
        }
        top++;

        // print the right column
        for (int i = top; i <= bottom; i++) {
            System.out.print(matrix[i][right] + " ");
        }
        right--;

        // print the bottom row
        for (int i = right; i >= left; i--) {
            System.out.print(matrix[bottom][i] + " ");
        }
        bottom--;

        // print the left column
        for (int i = bottom; i >= top; i--) {
            System.out.print(matrix[i][left] + " ");
        }
        left++;
    }
}
```
Explanation:

The solution uses a single pass through the matrix, with four nested loops that iterate over the rows and columns of the matrix. The outermost loop iterates over each row in the matrix, while the innermost loop iterates over each element in the row.

The loop variables `top`, `bottom`, `left`, and `right` are used to keep track of the boundaries of the current square that is being traversed. The loops iterate over the rows and columns of the matrix in a spiral fashion, starting from the top-left corner and moving clockwise.

For each row, the loop prints the elements of that row in the order they appear in the matrix. For example, for the first row, the elements are printed as `1 2 3 4`. The same applies to the other rows.

The time complexity of this solution is O(MxN), where M and N are the dimensions of the matrix. This is because each element in the matrix is only visited once during the traversal, regardless of its position in the matrix.

A hash table is a data structure that maps keys to values using a hash function. The purpose of a hash table is to store and retrieve data in an efficient manner by allowing for fast lookups, insertions, and deletions of key-value pairs.

The time complexity of various operations performed on a hash table can be analyzed as follows:

1. Lookup: Finding a value in the hash table based on its key is an O(1) operation, since the hash function returns an index into the array that is used to store the key-value pairs.
2. Insertion: Inserting a new key-value pair into the hash table can be done in O(1) time if the hash function produces a unique index for each key. If the hash function produces collisions, insertion takes O(n) time, where n is the number of keys that have the same hash value as the inserted key.
3. Deletion: Deleting a key-value pair from the hash table can be done in O(1) time if the hash function produces unique indices for each key. If the hash function produces collisions, deletion takes O(n) time, where n is the number of keys that have the same hash value as the deleted key.
4. Resizing: When the hash table grows or shrinks in size due to changes in the number of elements stored in it, all existing key-value pairs must be rehashed and inserted into the new array. This process can take O(n) time, where n is the number of keys in the hash table.

